OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026", "title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "032", "family_title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "abstract_html": "We prove the rational Hodge conjecture for complex abelian varieties with complex multiplication: every rational Hodge class on such a variety is a rational linear combination of algebraic cycle classes. As consequences, we obtain the generalized Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties, the Tate conjecture for abelian varieties over finite fields, and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every complex CM abelian variety \\(A\\) and every integer \\(p\\geq 0\\), the Betti cycle-class map \\( \\operatorname{cl}_{B}:\\operatorname{CH}^{p}(A)_{\\mathbb Q} \\longrightarrow H^{2p}(A,\\mathbb Q)\\cap H^{p,p}(A) \\) is …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A complex abelian variety \\(A\\) is of CM type if \\(\\operatorname{End}(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q\\) contains a commutative semisimple \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-algebra of dimension \\(2\\dim A\\). The point is included by convention.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\operatorname{CH}^{p}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}=\\operatorname{CH}^{p}(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every complex CM abelian variety \\(A\\) and every integer \\(p\\geq 0\\), the Betti cycle-class map\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cl}_{B}:\\operatorname{CH}^{p}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\longrightarrow\nH^{2p}(A,\\mathbb Q)\\cap H^{p,p}(A)\n\\]\nis surjective, where the intersection is taken in \\(H^{2p}(A,\\mathbb C)\\).</p><p>In particular, the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on every finite product of complex CM abelian varieties and on every power of such a product.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 18 proves the four-factor assertion for every Galois CM field. Proposition 2 consequently places every rational Hodge class on a CM abelian variety in its rational cycle span. The final scalar descent occurs on the original variety.</p><p>Finite products and powers are CM by Proposition 1, so the same conclusion applies to them.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a closed algebraic subset \\(Z\\subset A\\) of codimension at least \\(r\\) such that \\( W\\subset \\ker\\!\\left(H^{k}(A,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^{k}(A\\setminus Z,\\mathbb Q)\\right). \\) This includes every finite …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex CM abelian variety.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k,r\\geq 0\\) be integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\subset H^{k}(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) be a rational Hodge substructure.</p><p class=\"li\">The Tate convention is\n\\[\nV(r)^{a,b}=V^{a+r,b+r}.\n\\]\nA Hodge structure is effective if all its nonzero Hodge components have nonnegative indices.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(W(r)\\) is effective.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a closed algebraic subset \\(Z\\subset A\\) of codimension at least \\(r\\) such that\n\\[\nW\\subset\n\\ker\\!\\left(H^{k}(A,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^{k}(A\\setminus Z,\\mathbb Q)\\right).\n\\]\nThis includes every finite product of CM abelian varieties and every power of such a product.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every Hodge substructure of a CM abelian variety's cohomology is CM: its Hodge-circle group is an image of the torus acting on degree-one cohomology.</p><p>If \\(W=0\\), take \\(Z=\\varnothing\\). Otherwise semisimplicity of polarizable rational Hodge structures reduces to a simple summand, after which take a finite union of supports.</p><p>For a simple summand, let \\(r_0\\) be its smallest Hodge index. Effectivity of \\(W(r)\\) gives \\(r_0\\geq r\\). Set \\(n=k-2r_0\\), choose \\(B\\) from Proposition 19, and write \\(b=\\dim B\\). Composing its surjection with the inclusion of \\(W\\) gives\n\\[\nf:H^n(B,\\mathbb Q)(-r_0)\\longrightarrow H^k(A,\\mathbb Q),\n\\qquad\\operatorname{im}f=W.\n\\]\nPoincaré duality identifies \\(f\\) with a rational Hodge class of type \\((b+r_0,b+r_0)\\) in\n\\[\nH^{2b-n}(B,\\mathbb Q)\\otimes H^k(A,\\mathbb Q)\n\\subset H^{2b+2r_0}(B\\times A,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nThe product is CM. Theorem 1 represents this exact class by\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{b+r_0}(B\\times A)_{\\mathbb Q},\n\\]\nwhose degree-\\(n\\) action is \\(f\\).</p><p>Choose a finite rational cycle representative and let \\(Z\\) be the projection of its support to \\(A\\). Every component has dimension\n\\[\n\\dim(B\\times A)-(b+r_0)=\\dim A-r_0.\n\\]\nProper projection makes \\(Z\\) closed with codimension at least \\(r_0\\geq r\\). The correspondence restricts to zero on \\(B\\times(A\\setminus Z)\\), so its image \\(W\\) restricts to zero on \\(A\\setminus Z\\).</p><p>Finite dimensionality permits one finite union of supports for all summands. This is the geometric coniveau assertion\n\\[\nW\\subset N^rH^k(A,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 8.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The cycle-class map \\( \\operatorname{CH}^{r}(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q_\\ell \\longrightarrow H_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}^{2r}(\\overline A,\\mathbb Q_\\ell(r))^{G_q} \\) is surjective.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/\\mathbb F_q\\) be an abelian variety.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\overline A=A\\times_{\\mathbb F_q}\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\) and \\(G_q=\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q/\\mathbb F_q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\geq 0\\) and let \\(\\ell\\neq\\operatorname{char}(\\mathbb F_q)\\) be prime.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cycle-class map\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CH}^{r}(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q_\\ell\n\\longrightarrow\nH_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}^{2r}(\\overline A,\\mathbb Q_\\ell(r))^{G_q}\n\\]\nis surjective.</p><p>Numerical equivalence and \\(\\ell\\)-adic homological equivalence coincide on rational algebraic cycles on \\(\\overline A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Milne's Theorem 7.1 and formulation (0.1) in [25], with the correction [22]: enlarge the cofinal auxiliary Galois CM fields to contain a real subfield of degree greater than two inert at the residue prime. Theorem 1 supplies its Hodge-conjecture hypothesis.</p><p>Milne's theorem gives equality of numerical and \\(\\ell\\)-adic homological equivalence and algebraic spanning of geometric Tate classes, namely classes fixed over some finite field of definition.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\alpha\\in H_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}^{2r}(\\overline A,\\mathbb Q_\\ell(r))^{G_q}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\alpha\\) as a finite \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\)-linear combination of geometric cycle classes. All cycles descend to \\(A_{\\mathbb F_{q^m}}\\) for one \\(m\\). Let\n\\[\nz_m\\in\\operatorname{CH}^r(A_{\\mathbb F_{q^m}})\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q_\\ell\n\\]\nbe that combination, and let \\(\\pi_m:A_{\\mathbb F_{q^m}}\\to A\\) be projection. Proper pushforward and base change give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cl}_\\ell\\!\\left(\\frac1m(\\pi_m)_*z_m\\right)\n=\n\\frac1m\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{q^m}/\\mathbb F_q)}\n\\sigma(\\alpha)\n=\\alpha.\n\\]\nDivision by \\(m\\) is valid in \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\), even when \\(m\\) is divisible by \\(\\ell\\) or the characteristic.</p><p>Milne's implication compares CM and finite-field abelian motives; it does not assert that every Tate class lifts to a Hodge class on one CM lifting, even up to isogeny.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every prime \\(\\ell\\neq\\operatorname{char}(K)\\), numerical and \\(\\ell\\)-adic homological equivalence coincide on rational algebraic cycles on \\(A\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be an algebraically closed field and \\(A/K\\) an abelian variety of dimension \\(g\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(A_{\\mathrm{num}}^{r}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) for rational codimension-\\(r\\) cycles modulo numerical equivalence.</p><p class=\"li\">A rational ample divisor class is an \\(h\\in A_{\\mathrm{num}}^{1}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) for which some positive integral multiple is the numerical class of an ample Cartier divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\leq r\\leq g/2\\), put\n\\[\nP_h^{r}(A)=\n\\ker\\!\\left(\nh^{g-2r+1}:A_{\\mathrm{num}}^{r}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\longrightarrow A_{\\mathrm{num}}^{g-r+1}(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\right).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every prime \\(\\ell\\neq\\operatorname{char}(K)\\), numerical and \\(\\ell\\)-adic homological equivalence coincide on rational algebraic cycles on \\(A\\).</p><p>For every rational ample divisor class \\(h\\) and every integer \\(0\\leq r\\leq g/2\\), the rational symmetric form\n\\[\nP_h^{r}(A)\\times P_h^{r}(A)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Q,\n\\qquad\n(x,y)\\longmapsto (-1)^r\\deg(xyh^{g-2r})\n\\]\nis positive definite after extension of scalars to \\(\\mathbb R\\).</p><p>Thus the Hodge standard conjecture for \\(\\ell\\)-adic étale cohomology holds for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Milne's Theorem 3.3 [26] using Theorem 1. In that proof, the same Hodge hypothesis supplies compatibility and positivity of polarizations through Milne's Theorem 2.1, read with the addendum [23] and corrected auxiliary-field choice [22].</p><p>The result gives equality of numerical and \\(\\ell\\)-adic homological equivalence and positivity on the primitive space modulo homological equivalence. The equality identifies that primitive space with the numerical primitive space in the statement.</p><p>The case \\(g=0\\) is immediate. For \\(g&gt;0\\), choose \\(m&gt;0\\) so \\(mh\\) is the class of a smooth hyperplane section: clear denominators, take a sufficiently large very ample multiple, and use Bertini. The primitive kernel is unchanged because multiplication by \\(m^{g-2r+1}\\) is nonzero. The form for \\(mh\\) is \\(m^{g-2r}\\) times the form for \\(h\\), a positive factor, so positive definiteness is preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Remark 8.5, algebraicity of specialized Hodge classes", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every rational Hodge class on \\(A\\) specializes to the class of one rational algebraic cycle on \\(A_0\\), simultaneously in all prime-to-\\(p\\) realizations and in crystalline cohomology after extension to the completed …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a prime \\(p\\) and a \\(p\\)-adic place of \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) be an abelian variety having good reduction \\(A_0/\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\) at that place.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the specialization and realization conventions of the paper's reference [32, Corollaries 3.4 and 6.4], including \\(p=2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every rational Hodge class on \\(A\\) specializes to the class of one rational algebraic cycle on \\(A_0\\), simultaneously in all prime-to-\\(p\\) realizations and in crystalline cohomology after extension to the completed field at the chosen place.</p><p>This asserts algebraicity on the special fiber, not the Hodge conjecture for arbitrary complex abelian varieties.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the CM transport in [32, Corollary 3.4]. It writes the specialization of the given Hodge class as the pullback of a specialized CM Hodge class by a rational homomorphism of special fibers.</p><p>Theorem 1 algebraizes the CM Hodge class. Specialize that rational algebraic cycle, clear the rational homomorphism's denominator, and apply Chow pullback. This gives one rational algebraic cycle representing the transported specialization in all the stated realizations simultaneously.</p><p>The field-of-definition argument and realization conventions, including \\(p=2\\), are those of [32, Corollary 6.4]. This uses that paper's CM transport, not its independent rationality proof, and is not an input to Theorem 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(U(v)\\) is realized as \\(H^1(B(v),\\mathbb Q)\\) for a CM abelian variety \\(B(v)\\) of dimension \\(s\\).", "statement": "<p>CM embedding-line calculus: original Lemmas 2.1–2.4 and the construction surrounding Equation (2.2).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Cohomology is Betti cohomology unless otherwise specified.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\subset\\mathbb C\\), and write \\(T_k=T\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}k\\) for a rational vector space \\(T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth projective complex variety \\(X\\), write\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hdg}^{2p}(X)=H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\\cap H^{p,p}(X),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal Z^{2p}(X)=\n\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(\\operatorname{CH}^{p}(X)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\longrightarrow H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\\right).\n\\]\nA class with coefficients in \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) or \\(\\mathbb C\\) is called algebraic if it belongs to the corresponding scalar extension of \\(\\mathcal Z^{2p}(X)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\subset\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) be a Galois CM field, let \\(F=E^+\\), let \\(G_E=\\operatorname{Gal}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\), and let \\(c\\in G_E\\) be complex conjugation. Put \\(s=[F:\\mathbb Q]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An odd sign function is a function \\(v:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) satisfying \\(v(c\\tau)=-v(\\tau)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U(v)\\) be the rational vector space \\(E\\), made into a weight-one Hodge structure by declaring\n\\[\nU(v)_\\lambda=\n\\{u\\in U(v)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}:\nau=\\lambda(a)u\\text{ for every }a\\in E\\}\n\\]\nto have type \\((1,0)\\) if \\(v(\\lambda)=1\\), and type \\((0,1)\\) otherwise.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every \\(U(v)\\) is realized as \\(H^1(B(v),\\mathbb Q)\\) for a CM abelian variety \\(B(v)\\) of dimension \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The degree-one cohomology of a CM abelian variety is a direct sum of rank-one Hodge modules over CM fields. Finite products and powers of CM abelian varieties are CM.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Embedding lines with the same complete type function admit arbitrary zero-extended \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-linear transfers in the scalar extension of the rational Hodge maps. For abelian degree-one cohomology, these transfers are algebraic-coefficient linear combinations of pullbacks of abelian morphisms, up to rational denominators.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Every class in \\(H^2(X,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})\\) whose scalar conjugates all have type \\((1,1)\\) is algebraic, and\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal Z^{2p}(X)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\bigr)\n\\cap H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)=\\mathcal Z^{2p}(X).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.3 and 1.4 give the rank-one description and realization. Finite products are CM because the circle on the direct sum of their degree-one cohomologies lies in a product of tori, and its connected rational envelope is a subtorus.</p><p>A common Galois CM field exists for any finite collection of CM fields: take the compositum of all their conjugates. The identities \\(\\rho c_K=c_{\\mathbb C}\\rho\\) imply that complex conjugation is central in its Galois group; its fixed field is totally real.</p><p>Lemma 1.5 gives the transfer assertion. Lemma 1.1 and the Lefschetz \\((1,1)\\) theorem give algebraicity of every all-conjugate \\((1,1)\\) class.</p><p>Finally, extend a rational basis of \\(\\mathcal Z^{2p}(X)\\) to a basis of \\(H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\\) and compare coefficients. This gives the scalar-intersection identity. Scalar conjugation preserves algebraicity on the same complex variety because it fixes each rational cycle class and changes only its coefficient.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, scalar descent", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( t\\in (T\\cap P)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}. \\) The same assertion applies to the subspace of maps of any fixed Hodge type in a Hom space.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finite-dimensional rational vector space.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\subset T_{\\mathbb C}\\) be a complex-linear subspace.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t\\in T_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\), and assume \\(\\sigma t\\in P\\) for every \\(\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}/\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nt\\in (T\\cap P)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nThe same assertion applies to the subspace of maps of any fixed Hodge type in a Hom space.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a finite Galois extension \\(L/\\mathbb Q\\) containing the coefficients of \\(t\\). For \\(a\\in L\\), the vector\n\\[\nT_a=\\operatorname{Tr}_{L/\\mathbb Q}(at)\n=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(L/\\mathbb Q)}\n\\sigma(a)\\sigma(t)\n\\]\nis rational. Each automorphism of \\(L\\) extends to \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\), so every summand belongs to \\(P\\); hence \\(T_a\\in T\\cap P\\).</p><p>If \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\) is a rational basis of \\(L\\) and \\(b_1^*,\\ldots,b_m^*\\) its trace-dual basis, write \\(t=\\sum_j t_jb_j\\), with \\(t_j\\in T\\). Then \\(T_{b_j^*}=t_j\\), proving the assertion. The condition that a complex-linear map have a fixed Hodge type is complex-linear, so the same argument applies in a Hom space.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, realization in degree one", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Pullback induces an isomorphism \\( \\operatorname{Hom}(A,B)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q \\simeq \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}} \\bigl(H^1(B,\\mathbb Q),H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\bigr). \\) If …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B\\) be complex abelian varieties.</p><p class=\"li\">For the second assertion, let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected projective complex variety.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Pullback induces an isomorphism\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(A,B)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}\n\\bigl(H^1(B,\\mathbb Q),H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\nh:H^1(B,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^1(S,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nis a rational Hodge map, there are an integer \\(m\\geq1\\) and a morphism \\(f:S\\to B\\) such that \\(f^*=mh\\) on \\(H^1(B,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Dualizing a rational Hodge map gives a rational map on homology commuting with the complex structures. Multiplication by a positive integer makes it carry the integral lattice of \\(A\\) into that of \\(B\\). It consequently induces a holomorphic homomorphism of complex tori \\(A^{\\mathrm{an}}\\to B^{\\mathrm{an}}\\), which is algebraic. Conversely, an algebraic homomorphism induces the indicated Hodge pullback. This is the dual of the rational form of Riemann's theorem cited in [27, Proposition 6.5, Theorems 6.7–6.8, and Corollary 6.9].</p><p>The Albanese map induces\n\\[\nH^1(\\operatorname{Alb}S,\\mathbb Q)\\simeq H^1(S,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nApply the first assertion to the map with target \\(H^1(\\operatorname{Alb}S,\\mathbb Q)\\), clear a denominator, and compose \\(\\operatorname{Alb}S\\to B\\) with an Albanese map.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The following conditions are equivalent:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety and \\(H=H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a polarizable weight-one rational Hodge structure \\(H\\), let \\(J(H)\\) be the smallest rational algebraic subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}(H)\\) containing the Hodge circle. The circle acts by \\(z\\) on \\(H^{1,0}\\) and by \\(\\overline z\\) on \\(H^{0,1}\\), for \\(|z|=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following conditions are equivalent:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{(i)}&amp;J(H)\\text{ is a torus};\\\\\n\\text{(ii)}&amp;H\\text{ is a direct sum of rational Hodge structures, each rank one}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{over a CM field acting by Hodge endomorphisms};\\\\\n\\text{(iii)}&amp;\\operatorname{End}(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q\n\\text{ contains a commutative semisimple algebra}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{of rational dimension }2\\dim A.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The zero-dimensional case is immediate. The Hodge circle preserves an alternating polarization. Its connectedness and the minimality of \\(J(H)\\) imply that \\(J(H)\\) is connected.</p><p>Assume (i). Its associative envelope \\(E_0\\subset\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb Q}(H)\\) is commutative semisimple: after splitting the torus, it is a reduced subalgebra of a product of scalar fields. This description descends to \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p>Let \\(*\\) be the polarization adjoint. It preserves \\(E_0\\), since it sends symplectic torus elements to their inverses. Every element of \\(E_0\\) commutes with the complex structure supplied by the Hodge circle. Thus \\(*\\) is also the adjoint for the positive definite real form obtained by inserting that complex structure into the polarization. In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_{\\mathbb R}}(a^*a)&gt;0\n\\qquad(0\\neq a\\in E_0\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R).\n\\]\nThe involution cannot exchange primitive real factors: an idempotent \\(e\\) in one exchanged factor would satisfy \\(e^*e=0\\). On a complex factor it cannot be the identity, because \\((ie)^*(ie)=-e\\) has negative trace. Hence it acts by complex conjugation on every complex factor. There are no real factors: the circle would act there through a continuous homomorphism to \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\), hence trivially, contrary to the weight-one characters.</p><p>Every rational field factor of \\(E_0\\) is therefore CM. Its involution becomes complex conjugation at every embedding, and its fixed field is totally real of index two. The circle acts on the corresponding field module by scalar multiplication. Splitting that module into field lines gives rational Hodge substructures, proving (ii), with multiplicities retained.</p><p>The separate scalar actions on the field lines form a commutative semisimple Hodge-endomorphism algebra of dimension \\(\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}H\\). Lemma 1.2 realizes it in \\(\\operatorname{End}(A)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\\), proving (iii).</p><p>Conversely, write an algebra in (iii) as \\(\\prod_j K_j\\). Its faithful action on \\(H\\) has positive ranks \\(m_j\\), and\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}H=\\sum_jm_j[K_j:\\mathbb Q]\n=\\sum_j[K_j:\\mathbb Q].\n\\]\nEvery \\(m_j=1\\). Its centralizer in \\(\\operatorname{GL}(H)\\) is the torus\n\\[\n\\prod_j\\operatorname{Res}_{K_j/\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb G_m.\n\\]\nThe Hodge circle lies in this torus because the algebra acts by Hodge endomorphisms. Hence \\(J(H)\\) is a torus.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Section 2.2, including Equations (2.2) and (2.3)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Hodge structure \\(U(v)\\) is polarizable and is realized by a CM abelian variety \\(B(v)\\) of dimension \\(s\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E,F,G_E,c,s,v,U(v)\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Hodge structure \\(U(v)\\) is polarizable and is realized by a CM abelian variety \\(B(v)\\) of dimension \\(s\\):\n\\[\nU(v)=H^1(B(v),\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}/\\mathbb Q)\\) restricts to \\(g\\in G_E\\), then\n\\[\n\\sigma U(v)_\\lambda=U(v)_{g\\lambda}.\n\\]\nThus the complete type functions of \\(U(v)_1\\) and \\(U(v)_c\\) are \\(v\\) and \\(-v\\), respectively.</p><p>The polarization and realization construction also applies to a non-Galois CM field and an arbitrary CM type.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(0\\neq\\delta\\in E\\) with \\(c(\\delta)=-\\delta\\). Multiplying \\(\\delta\\) by an element of \\(F^\\times\\), weak approximation gives \\(\\xi\\in E^\\times\\) such that\n\\[\nc(\\xi)=-\\xi,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sgn}(\\operatorname{Im}\\tau(\\xi))=-v(\\tau)\n\\]\nat one representative of each conjugate pair. The form\n\\[\n\\psi_v(x,y)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{E/\\mathbb Q}(\\xi x c(y))\n\\]\nis alternating and nondegenerate. It pairs only conjugate embedding lines. For a nonzero vector \\(u\\) in the sum of the \\((1,0)\\) lines, \\(i\\psi_v(u,\\overline u)\\) is a sum of positive multiples of squared absolute values of its embedding coordinates. This is the weight-one polarization condition.</p><p>After scaling, the form is integral on a lattice. The Riemann criterion, applied to the dual homological Hodge structure, gives \\(B(v)\\). This permits imprimitive CM types and does not require a principal polarization. The argument did not otherwise require the Galois hypothesis.</p><p>For scalar conjugation, rationality of the \\(E\\)-action gives, for \\(u\\in U(v)_\\lambda\\),\n\\[\na\\,\\sigma(u)=\\sigma(au)\n=\\sigma(\\lambda(a))\\sigma(u)\n=(g\\lambda)(a)\\sigma(u).\n\\]\nThis proves the asserted action on labels.</p><p>For an embedding line in a rank-one module over a CM subfield of \\(E\\), choose \\(\\lambda\\in G_E\\) extending its label. Its complete type function is \\(+1\\) at \\(g\\) when the line labeled by the restriction of \\(g\\lambda\\) is holomorphic, and \\(-1\\) otherwise. It is odd and records the types of all scalar conjugates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, transfer of an embedding line", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( T\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(H,H') \\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}. \\) For abelian degree-one cohomologies, \\(T\\) is consequently an algebraic-coefficient linear combination of pullbacks of abelian …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H,H'\\) be direct sums of rank-one weight-one Hodge modules over CM fields contained in a Galois CM field \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\subset H_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\) and \\(M\\subset H'_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\) be embedding lines with the same complete type function.</p><p class=\"li\">Extend a \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-linear map \\(T:L\\to M\\) by zero on all other source embedding lines.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nT\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(H,H')\n\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nFor abelian degree-one cohomologies, \\(T\\) is consequently an algebraic-coefficient linear combination of pullbacks of abelian morphisms, up to rational denominators.</p>", "proof": "<p>Scalar conjugation transports the entire source and target decompositions. For each \\(\\sigma\\), the only possibly nonzero component of \\(\\sigma T\\) maps \\(\\sigma L\\) to \\(\\sigma M\\), whose types agree. All other components are zero. Thus every scalar conjugate is a Hodge map on the whole source. Apply Lemma 1.1 in \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb Q}(H,H')\\), followed by Lemma 1.2 when applicable.</p><p>The map need not be invariant under a stabilizer of either line. The argument retains repeated rational summands separately.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.6, reduction to four factors", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under this assumption, every rational Hodge class on every complex CM abelian variety is algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For every Galois CM field \\(E\\), use the structures \\(U(v)=H^1(B(v),\\mathbb Q)\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the following four-factor assertion: whenever odd sign functions \\(v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4\\) satisfy \\(v_1+v_2=v_3+v_4\\), the line\n\\[\nU(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c\n\\subset\nH^4\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4B(v_i),\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\right)\n\\]\nis algebraic.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under this assumption, every rational Hodge class on every complex CM abelian variety is algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be CM and \\(h\\in\\operatorname{Hdg}^{2p}(A)\\). For \\(p=0,1\\), use the unit class and the Lefschetz \\((1,1)\\) theorem.</p><p>By Proposition 1, decompose \\(H=H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) into rank-one CM-field modules, keeping repeated summands separate. Choose a Galois CM field \\(E\\) containing the fields and their conjugates. Over \\(E\\), \\(H\\) splits into embedding lines. Since\n\\[\nH^*(A,\\mathbb Q)=\\Lambda^*H,\n\\]\nthe exterior monomials in these lines give a direct-sum decomposition into one-dimensional lines, each of a single Hodge bidegree.</p><p>Scalar conjugation permutes these monomial lines, with only possible reordering signs. Rationality of \\(h\\) implies that a supported line has every scalar conjugate supported; all therefore have type \\((p,p)\\). Its components have algebraic Betti coefficients.</p><p>Fix a supported monomial, and let \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_{2p}\\) be its factors' complete type functions. Its all-conjugate balance is precisely\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{2p}z_i=0.\n\\]\nSet \\(x_i=z_i\\) and \\(y_i=-z_{p+i}\\), for \\(1\\leq i\\leq p\\). Then \\(\\sum_i x_i=\\sum_i y_i\\). For an ordered list \\(a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)\\), put\n\\[\nA_a=\\prod_{i=1}^pB(a_i),\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_{a,b}=\n\\bigotimes_{i=1}^pU(a_i)_1\\otimes\n\\bigotimes_{i=1}^pU(b_i)_c\n\\subset H^{2p}(A_a\\times A_b,\\overline{\\mathbb Q}).\n\\]\nEmbedding-line transfers reduce the monomial to algebraicity of \\(\\mathcal L_{x,y}\\).</p><p>Regard each list as a matrix whose columns are representatives of \\(G_E/\\langle c\\rangle\\). Equality of sums means equality of the number of plus signs in each column. In a column, pair the mismatches \\((+1,-1)\\) with the mismatches \\((-1,+1)\\), and swap the current entries in each pair of rows, making the opposite swap at the conjugate coordinate. This corrects the column, leaves other columns unchanged, and preserves oddness. It gives a finite sequence\n\\[\nx=x^{(0)},x^{(1)},\\ldots,x^{(N)}=y\n\\]\nwhose consecutive lists differ by one two-row swap.</p><p>The old and new rows satisfy \\(a+b=a'+b'\\). The assumed four-factor assertion gives an algebraic generator of\n\\[\nU(a)_1\\otimes U(b)_1\\otimes U(a')_c\\otimes U(b')_c.\n\\]\nJoin each unchanged row to its new copy by the all-conjugate \\((1,1)\\) line \\(U(v)_1\\otimes U(v)_c\\). Exterior product gives a nonzero algebraic generator of each transition line. If the lists agree initially, these two-factor tensors suffice.</p><p>To compose transitions, write pure algebraic generators as\n\\[\nt_{ab}=\\alpha_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\alpha_p\n\\otimes\\beta_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\beta_p,\n\\quad\nt_{be}=\\gamma_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\gamma_p\n\\otimes\\delta_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\delta_p,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\beta_i\\in U(b_i)_c\\) and \\(\\gamma_i\\in U(b_i)_1\\). Choose ample divisor classes \\(\\ell_i\\) on \\(B(b_i)\\), and let \\(r_{ab},r_{be},r_{ae}\\) be the projections from \\(A_a\\times A_b\\times A_e\\). Cup product and pushforward give the original Equation (2.6):\n\\[\n(r_{ae})_*\n\\left(r_{ab}^*t_{ab}\\smile r_{be}^*t_{be}\n\\smile\\prod_{i=1}^p\\ell_i^{s-1}\\right)\n=\n(-1)^{p(p-1)/2}\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^pq_i\\right)t_{ae},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nq_i=\\int_{B(b_i)}\\beta_i\\wedge\\gamma_i\\wedge\\ell_i^{s-1},\n\\qquad\nt_{ae}=\\alpha_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\alpha_p\n\\otimes\\delta_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\delta_p.\n\\]\nThe sign comes from regrouping the middle degree-one factors. Complex conjugation exchanges their embedding lines, so \\(\\gamma_i\\) is a nonzero scalar multiple of \\(\\overline{\\beta_i}\\). Hodge–Riemann positivity gives \\(q_i\\neq0\\), without requiring compatibility between \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(E\\). Since the Betti coefficients are algebraic, \\(q_i\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}^{\\times}\\). The codimension is\n\\[\np+p+p(s-1)-ps=p.\n\\]\nDivision by the nonzero algebraic scalar proves algebraicity of the endpoint line. Iterate to obtain \\(\\mathcal L_{x,y}\\).</p><p>Transfer from the auxiliary \\(U\\)'s to \\(H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\). Each transfer is an algebraic-coefficient combination of rational pullbacks along morphisms \\(A\\to B(v)\\). Apply their exterior product on \\(A^{2p}\\), then pull back by the diagonal \\(A\\to A^{2p}\\). The result is the original monomial's cup product, nonzero because its factors occupy distinct embedding or multiplicity lines. Thus every supported monomial line is algebraic. Sum the components of \\(h\\), and apply the scalar-intersection identity of Proposition 1.</p><p>The construction uses auxiliary products and contractions; it does not assert generation of a fixed power's Hodge ring by cup products of classes of codimensions one and two.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 2.7, choice of the distinguished embedding", "depth": 0, "snippet": "To establish algebraicity of every four-factor switch line \\( U(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c, \\) it suffices to establish it when \\(s\\geq2\\) and \\(v_i(1)=1\\) for all four functions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be Galois CM, \\(F=E^+\\), \\(s=[F:\\mathbb Q]\\), and \\(c\\) its complex conjugation.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd and satisfy \\(v_1+v_2=v_3+v_4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>To establish algebraicity of every four-factor switch line\n\\[\nU(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c,\n\\]\nit suffices to establish it when \\(s\\geq2\\) and \\(v_i(1)=1\\) for all four functions.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(v_1,v_2\\) are nowhere simultaneously positive, oddness also excludes simultaneous negativity. Thus \\(v_2=-v_1\\), and the relation gives \\(v_4=-v_3\\). The first two and last two slots are separately all-conjugate \\((1,1)\\) pairs, hence algebraic by Proposition 1.</p><p>Otherwise choose \\(g\\in G_E\\) with \\(v_1(g)=v_2(g)=1\\). The relation makes all four signs positive there. A scalar automorphism extending \\(g\\) sends the labels to \\(g,g,gc,gc\\). Define the right translates\n\\[\nv_i^g(\\tau)=v_i(\\tau g).\n\\]\nThey are odd, satisfy \\(v_i^g(1)=1\\), and retain the relation. Centrality of \\(c\\) in \\(G_E\\) makes the complete type of the \\(gc\\)-line equal to \\(-v_i^g\\). Proposition 1 transfers the normalized switch to the lines labeled \\(g,g,gc,gc\\). Apply the inverse scalar automorphism.</p><p>For \\(s=1\\), there is only one odd sign function positive at the identity, and the normalized tensor is a product of ordinary pairs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 3.1, surface criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The four-factor switch line \\( U(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c \\) is algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be Galois CM with conjugation \\(c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v_i:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq4\\), be odd and satisfy \\(v_1+v_2=v_3+v_4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\((\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2,\\lambda_3,\\lambda_4)=(1,1,c,c)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected projective complex surface.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose there are rational Hodge maps\n\\[\nh_i:U(v_i)\\longrightarrow H^1(S,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nand nonzero \\(\\alpha_i\\in U(v_i)_{\\lambda_i}\\subset U(v_i)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\int_S h_1(\\alpha_1)\\smile h_2(\\alpha_2)\n\\smile h_3(\\alpha_3)\\smile h_4(\\alpha_4)\\neq0,\n\\]\nwhere the maps are extended to \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-coefficients.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The four-factor switch line\n\\[\nU(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c\n\\]\nis algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 1, choose integers \\(m_i&gt;0\\) and morphisms \\(f_i:S\\to B(v_i)\\) with \\(f_i^*=m_ih_i\\). Then\n\\[\nk=\\int_Sf_1^*\\alpha_1\\smile f_2^*\\alpha_2\n\\smile f_3^*\\alpha_3\\smile f_4^*\\alpha_4\n\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}^{\\times}.\n\\]\nIts coefficients are algebraic because pullback, cup product, and evaluation on \\([S]\\) are rational Betti operations.</p><p>Put \\(A=\\prod_iB(v_i)\\), \\(n=\\dim A=4s\\), \\(f=(f_i)\\), and\n\\[\nz=f_*[S]\\in\\operatorname{CH}^{n-2}(A),\\qquad\nZ=\\operatorname{cl}(z)\\in\\mathcal Z^{2n-4}(A).\n\\]\nFor \\(t_\\alpha=\\alpha_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\alpha_4\\), the projection formula gives\n\\[\n\\int_AZ\\smile t_\\alpha=k.\n\\]\nThus \\(Z\\) is a nonzero functional on the desired line, not itself a vector on that line. In particular, \\(f(S)\\) cannot have dimension less than two.</p><p>Take copies \\(B(v_i)'\\) with transported \\(E\\)-actions, and nonzero algebraic-Betti vectors\n\\[\n\\beta_i\\in U(v_i)'_{c\\lambda_i}.\n\\]\nThe pure class\n\\[\nD_i=\\alpha_i\\otimes\\beta_i\n\\in H^2(B(v_i)\\times B(v_i)',\\overline{\\mathbb Q})\n\\]\nhas type \\((1,1)\\ in every scalar conjugate: after an automorphism restricting to \\(g\\in G_E\\), its labels are \\(g\\lambda_i\\) and \\(gc\\lambda_i=cg\\lambda_i\\). Proposition 1 therefore makes \\(D_i\\) algebraic. This is the entire pure \\(H^1\\otimes H^1\\) class; no projection of an arbitrary divisor is needed.</p><p>Let \\(A'=\\prod_iB(v_i)'\\), let \\(p,p'\\) be the projections from \\(A\\times A'\\), and let \\(q_i\\) select the \\(i\\)-th old and new factors. Set\n\\[\nK=\\prod_{i=1}^4q_i^*D_i,\\qquad\nt_\\beta=\\beta_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\beta_4.\n\\]\nOrdering old factors before new factors makes \\(3+2+1=6\\) sign changes, so\n\\[\nK=t_\\alpha\\otimes t_\\beta.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\np'_*(p^*Z\\smile K)\n=\\left(\\int_AZ\\smile t_\\alpha\\right)t_\\beta\n=kt_\\beta.\n\\]\nOther Künneth components contribute zero. The codimension is\n\\[\n(n-2)+4-n=2.\n\\]\nThe left side is algebraic with \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-coefficients. Divide by \\(k\\), and obtain the algebraic line labeled \\((c,c,1,1)\\) on \\(A'\\). Scalar complex conjugation preserves the rational cycle span on the same variety and changes the labels to \\((1,1,c,c)\\). Identifying the copies with the original factors proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Corollary 3.2, surface criterion for complex spans", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The four-factor switch line is algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E,v_i,\\lambda_i,S\\) have the assumptions of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\eta_i\\in H^1(S,\\mathbb C)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\eta_i\\in\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\n\\{h(U(v_i)_{\\lambda_i}):\nh\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(U(v_i),H^1(S,\\mathbb Q))\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\n\\int_S\\eta_1\\smile\\eta_2\\smile\\eta_3\\smile\\eta_4\\neq0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The four-factor switch line is algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a nonzero algebraic Betti generator of each selected line. Each \\(\\eta_i\\) is a finite complex linear combination of its images under rational Hodge maps. Expanding the nonzero cup product selects a nonzero term satisfying Proposition 4. All four maps have the same target \\(H^1(S,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The line forms can be chosen so that \\(\\mathcal F_P\\) is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian.", "statement": "<p>Theta tensors, differential bundles, and congruence descent: original Lemmas 4.1, 4.2, 4.4, and Remark 4.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\subset\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) be Galois CM, \\(F=E^+\\), \\(s=[F:\\mathbb Q]\\geq2\\), and \\(G_E=\\operatorname{Gal}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix representatives \\(\\Sigma\\subset G_E\\) modulo complex conjugation \\(c\\), including \\(\\mu=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be an \\(E/F\\)-Hermitian three-space, with forms linear in the first argument, of signature \\((2,1)\\) at \\(\\mu\\) and positive definite at the other real places.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nG=\\operatorname{SU}(V),\\qquad\nD=\\{L\\subset V_\\mu:\\dim_{\\mathbb C}L=1,\\ h_V|_L&lt;0\\}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(L\\) for the tautological line and \\(Q=V_\\mu/L\\). Then\n\\[\nT_D=\\operatorname{Hom}(L,Q),\\qquad\n\\Omega_D^1=L\\otimes Q^*.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an \\(\\mathcal O_E\\)-lattice \\(\\Lambda\\subset V\\) and \\(\\delta\\in E^\\times\\) with \\(c(\\delta)=-\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A multiplicity line \\(W_i\\) has an odd sign function \\(d_i:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\), with \\(d_i(\\mu)=1\\). For a prescribed CM function \\(v_i\\), use \\(d_i(\\tau)=v_i(\\tau^{-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be one such line or a rational orthogonal sum of two such lines, and let \\(n=\\dim_EP\\in\\{1,2\\}\\). Put\n\\[\n\\mathbb V_P=\\operatorname{Res}_{E/F}(V\\otimes_EP),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\psi_P(v\\otimes p,v'\\otimes p')\n=\\operatorname{Tr}_{E/F}\n\\bigl(\\delta h_V(v,v')h_P(p,p')\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts underlying rational alternating form is \\(\\operatorname{Tr}_{F/\\mathbb Q}\\psi_P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a multiplicity line \\(W_i\\), prescribe\n\\[\n(\\mathcal F_i)_\\mu=L\\otimes(W_i)_\\mu,\\qquad\n(\\mathcal F_i)_{c\\mu}=Q^*\\otimes(W_i)_\\mu^*.\n\\]\nHere \\(Q^*=\\operatorname{ann}(L)\\subset V_\\mu^*\\), embedded in the conjugate summand by the Hermitian pairing. At \\(\\tau\\in\\Sigma\\setminus\\{\\mu\\}\\), take the full \\(c\\tau\\)-summand when \\(d_i(\\tau)=1\\), and the full \\(\\tau\\)-summand when \\(d_i(\\tau)=-1\\). For an orthogonal sum, take the direct sum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line forms can be chosen so that \\(\\mathcal F_P\\) is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian. Its determinant has an equivariant square root\n\\[\n\\det\\mathcal F_P\\simeq L^{2n}\\otimes C_P,\\qquad\n(\\det\\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\\simeq L^n\\otimes C_P^{1/2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_P\\) is a constant line with trivial \\(G(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\\)-action.</p><p>The completed theta Taylor tensors attached to finite Schwartz inputs take values in\n\\[\n(\\det\\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^k\\mathcal F_P.\n\\]\nTheir first tensors are holomorphic. Every finite list of inputs descends at a common sufficiently deep principal lattice congruence level.</p><p>For \\(n=1\\), projection of the first tensor to the \\(c\\mu\\)-summand gives a holomorphic one-form, after a complex trivialization of a constant line. For \\(n=2\\), double alternation of the projected second tensor has target \\(K_D\\) tensored with a constant line.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.3 give the bundle targets, rational covariance, and common congruence descent. For \\(n=1\\),\n\\[\n(\\det\\mathcal F_i)^{1/2}\\otimes(\\mathcal F_i)_{c\\mu}\n\\simeq L\\otimes Q^*\\otimes(\\text{constant line})\n\\simeq\\Omega_D^1\\otimes(\\text{constant line}).\n\\]\nFor \\(P=W_1\\perp W_2\\), define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{alt}_2:\n\\operatorname{Sym}^2(Q^*\\otimes P_\\mu^*)\n\\longrightarrow\\Lambda^2Q^*\\otimes\\Lambda^2P_\\mu^*,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{alt}_2((a\\otimes u)(b\\otimes v))\n=(a\\wedge b)\\otimes(u\\wedge v).\n\\]\nBoth exterior signs change under exchange, so it is well-defined. Its target, after multiplication by the half-determinant line, is\n\\[\nL^2\\otimes\\Lambda^2Q^*\\otimes(\\text{constant line})\n=K_D\\otimes(\\text{constant line}).\n\\]\nChoose nonzero complex constant-line vectors; no rational trivialization is asserted.</p><p>The quotients used here are compact. A vector isotropic over \\(E\\) would be isotropic at a definite real place, which is impossible. Equivalently, \\(G\\) has no nontrivial \\(F\\)-split torus: a nontrivial split weight in \\(V\\) would force isotropy. The arithmetic lattice and compactness theorems [6; 27] give cocompact lattices in\n\\[\nG(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{SU}(2,1)\\times\\prod_{\\tau\\neq\\mu}\\operatorname{SU}(3).\n\\]\nProjection past the compact factors preserves discreteness and cocompactness: a sequence approaching the identity in the active factor has a subsequence converging in the compact factors, whose differences contradict discreteness. A deep principal level is torsion-free and acts freely because a discrete subgroup meets a point stabilizer in a finite group. The quotient is a smooth connected compact Kähler surface.</p><p>Congruence descent uses the smooth finite action, not an assertion that every finite-index ball subgroup is congruence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Hermitian forms on the multiplicity lines can be chosen over \\(E/F\\) so that \\(\\mathcal F_P\\) is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian, equivariantly for \\(G(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\\), and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and prescribed subbundles of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\tau(\\delta)=it_\\tau\\), with \\(t_\\tau\\in\\mathbb R^\\times\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Positivity means\n\\[\n\\psi_{P,\\tau}(u,\\overline u)/i&gt;0\n\\quad\n(0\\neq u\\in(\\mathcal F_P)_\\tau\\oplus(\\mathcal F_P)_{c\\tau}).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Hermitian forms on the multiplicity lines can be chosen over \\(E/F\\) so that \\(\\mathcal F_P\\) is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian, equivariantly for \\(G(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\\), and\n\\[\n\\det\\mathcal F_P\\simeq L^{2n}\\otimes C_P,\\qquad\n(\\det\\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\\simeq L^n\\otimes C_P^{1/2},\n\\]\nwith \\(C_P\\) constant and \\(G(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\\)-trivial.</p>", "proof": "<p>The form \\(\\psi_P\\) is alternating because interchanging the arguments conjugates the Hermitian product and changes the sign of \\(\\delta\\). It is nondegenerate by nondegeneracy of the tensor-product Hermitian form and trace pairing: for nonzero Hermitian pairing value \\(b\\), multiplication of the second vector by \\(e\\in E\\) changes that value to \\(c(e)b\\), and trace duality supplies nonzero alternating pairing.</p><p>On a \\(\\tau\\)-summand the positivity sign is that of \\(t_\\tau h_Vh_P\\), and on the \\(c\\tau\\)-summand it is opposite. At \\(\\mu\\), choose each line coefficient with sign \\(-\\operatorname{sgn}(t_\\mu)\\). The negative line on the \\(\\mu\\)-side and dual positive plane on the \\(c\\mu\\)-side are then positive. At a definite place choose the coefficient with sign\n\\[\n-d_i(\\tau)\\operatorname{sgn}(t_\\tau).\n\\]\nWeak approximation in \\(F\\) realizes all these strict sign conditions.</p><p>The active subspaces are annihilators; elsewhere one side is full and the other zero. Hence the bundle is isotropic of half dimension. The annihilator description proves holomorphy, and the remaining summands are constant. Equivariance follows from preservation of \\(L\\), its annihilator, and the full embedding summands.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\det Q^*=L\\otimes\\det(V_\\mu)^*,\n\\]\nthe active determinant is\n\\[\n\\det(L\\otimes P_\\mu)\\otimes\\det(Q^*\\otimes P_\\mu^*)\n\\simeq\nL^{2n}\\otimes(\\det(V_\\mu)^*)^{\\otimes n}\\otimes\\det(P_\\mu)^*.\n\\]\nAll other factors are constant; their special-unitary actions are trivial because they involve determinant powers of \\(V_\\tau\\) or \\(V_\\tau^*\\), while \\(G\\) acts trivially on \\(P\\). Choose a square root of the constant line.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2; coordinate consequence: Remark 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\vartheta_{\\omega_f(\\widetilde g_f)\\varphi}(gZ,J^{-t}z) = j(g,Z) \\exp\\!\\left(\\pi i\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma} z_\\tau^tJ_\\tau^{-1}C_\\tau z_\\tau\\right)\\vartheta_\\varphi(Z,z), \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 6 and choose \\(F\\)-symplectic coordinates \\((p,q)\\in F^a\\oplus F^a\\), \\(a=3n\\), with\n\\[\n\\psi_P((p,q),(p',q'))=p^tq'-q^tp'.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write the positive Lagrangian as the graph frame\n\\[\ne_Z=\\binom ZI,\\qquad Z=Z^t,\\quad\\operatorname{Im}Z&gt;0\n\\]\nat each real place.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\varphi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb A_{F,f}^{a})\\), put\n\\[\n\\vartheta_\\varphi(Z,z)=\n\\sum_{x\\in F^a}\\varphi(x)\n\\exp\\!\\left(\n\\pi i\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}x_\\tau^tZ_\\tau x_\\tau\n+2\\pi i\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}x_\\tau^tz_\\tau\n\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widehat\\vartheta_\\varphi(Z,z)=\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac\\pi2\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}\nz_\\tau^t(\\operatorname{Im}Z_\\tau)^{-1}z_\\tau\\right)\n\\vartheta_\\varphi(Z,z)\n=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\langle T_{k,\\varphi}(Z),z^{\\otimes k}\\rangle.\n\\]\nThus \\(T_{k,\\varphi}\\) includes the factor \\(1/k!\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\in\\operatorname{Sp}(\\mathbb V_P)(F)\\), and at each real place write\n\\[\ng=\\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\\\ C&amp;D_0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\quad J=CZ+D_0,\\quad gZ=(AZ+B)J^{-1}.\n\\]\nChoose an infinite metaplectic lift with\n\\[\nj(g,Z)^2=\\prod_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}\\det J_\\tau,\n\\]\nand its paired finite lift \\(\\widetilde g_f\\) whose adelic product is Weil's rational lift.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\vartheta_{\\omega_f(\\widetilde g_f)\\varphi}(gZ,J^{-t}z)\n=\nj(g,Z)\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\pi i\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}\nz_\\tau^tJ_\\tau^{-1}C_\\tau z_\\tau\\right)\\vartheta_\\varphi(Z,z),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nT_{k,\\omega_f(\\widetilde g_f)\\varphi}(gZ)\n=j(g,Z)\\operatorname{Sym}^k(J)T_{k,\\varphi}(Z).\n\\]\nThe tensor target is\n\\[\n(\\det\\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^k\\mathcal F_P,\n\\]\nwith the positive Lagrangian, not its dual, in the symmetric factor. The first tensor is holomorphic.</p>", "proof": "<p>A positive Lagrangian cannot meet the \\(p\\)-axis nontrivially, since the alternating pairing with its conjugate would vanish. Projection to \\(q\\) is therefore an isomorphism. Isotropy gives symmetry of \\(Z\\), and\n\\[\n\\psi_{P,\\tau}(e_Zu,\\overline{e_Zu})/i\n=2u^t(\\operatorname{Im}Z)\\overline u\n\\]\ngives \\(\\operatorname{Im}Z&gt;0\\).</p><p>The theta series and derivatives converge uniformly on compact subsets. The rational support of \\(\\varphi\\) is contained in finitely many translates of a full Minkowski lattice. Derivatives are bounded by a polynomial times\n\\[\nC\\exp(-c\\|x\\|^2+C'\\|x\\|),\n\\]\nwhose lattice sum converges. For smooth parameters on a compact parameter set, the same holds provided the needed period-map derivatives are bounded and the imaginary parts have a common positive lower bound.</p><p>Take the global additive character with infinite component \\(t\\mapsto\\exp(2\\pi i\\operatorname{Tr}_{F/\\mathbb Q}t)\\). Weil's rational section fixes\n\\[\n\\lambda(\\Phi)=\\sum_{x\\in F^a}\\Phi(x).\n\\]\nHis adelic twofold subgroup contains the rational section [37, Chapters III–IV]. Thus infinite and finite double-cover lifts can be paired as stated; this does not assert splitting of an arbitrary adelic unitary double cover.</p><p>At one real place write\n\\[\nf_{Z,z}(x)=\\exp(\\pi ix^tZx+2\\pi ix^tz),\n\\qquad K(g,Z)=J^{-1}C.\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(K(g,Z)\\) is symmetric. The infinite formula is\n\\[\n\\omega_\\infty(\\widetilde g)f_{Z,z}\n=j(g,Z)^{-1}\\exp(-\\pi iz^tK(g,Z)z)\\,\nf_{gZ,J^{-t}z}.\n\\]\nFor \\(n(B)=\\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;B\\\\0&amp;I\\end{pmatrix}\\), it follows from multiplication by \\(\\exp(\\pi ix^tBx)\\). For \\(m(A)=\\operatorname{diag}(A,A^{-t})\\), use\n\\[\n\\omega_\\infty(\\widetilde m(A))b(x)=j(m(A))^{-1}b(A^tx),\n\\qquad j(m(A))^2=(\\det A)^{-1}.\n\\]\nFor positive determinant, \\(j^{-1}=|\\det A|^{1/2}\\); for negative determinant retain the metaplectic phase.</p><p>For \\(w=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-I\\\\I&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\), choose\n\\[\nb\\longmapsto e^{-\\pi ia/4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^a}b(y)e^{-2\\pi ix^ty}\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian integral is\n\\[\n\\det(-iZ)^{-1/2}\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\pi i(x-z)^tZ^{-1}(x-z)\\right).\n\\]\nTaking \\(j(w,Z)=e^{\\pi ia/4}\\det(-iZ)^{1/2}\\), with the square root equal to \\(1\\) at \\(Z=iI\\), gives \\(j(w,Z)^2=\\det Z\\).</p><p>These generators give the formula for the real symplectic group. The composition identities are\n\\[\nJ(g_1g_2,Z)=J_1J_2,\\qquad\nK(g_1g_2,Z)=K(g_2,Z)+J_2^{-1}K(g_1,g_2Z)J_2^{-t}.\n\\]\nThe second follows from \\(A_2-(g_2Z)C_2=J_2^{-t}\\). Multiply over all real places and apply rational invariance of \\(\\lambda\\). This gives the theta transformation law with \\(\\omega_f(\\widetilde g_f)\\), not its inverse, on the left.</p><p>For \\(Y=\\operatorname{Im}Z\\), \\(Y'=\\operatorname{Im}(gZ)\\), the symplectic identities give\n\\[\nY'=\\overline J^{-t}YJ^{-1},\\qquad\nJ^{-1}(Y')^{-1}J^{-t}=Y^{-1}-2iJ^{-1}C.\n\\]\nThe completion cancels the quadratic exponential. Comparing homogeneous terms gives the tensor law. Finally,\n\\[\nge_Z=e_{gZ}J,\n\\]\nso \\(J\\) acts on the positive Lagrangian. The completion has no linear term, and \\(T_{1,\\varphi}\\) is the ordinary holomorphic theta gradient.</p><p>For the original Remark 4.3, if a rational symplectic matrix \\(S\\) takes adapted coordinates to reference coordinates, then\n\\[\nZ_{\\mathrm{ref}}=SZ_{\\mathrm{ad}},\\qquad\n\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ad}}=\\omega_f(\\widetilde S_f)^{-1}\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}.\n\\]\nChanging the paired lift's sign changes both sides' signs compatibly. In coordinates adapted to a rational symplectic direct sum, the period matrix is block diagonal, and completed theta series of product inputs multiply.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The unitary splitting restricts to an honest smooth finite-adelic action \\(\\omega_{P,f}\\) of \\(G(\\mathbb A_{F,f})\\), compatible with Lemma 6.2 and independent of the splitting characters.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the symplectic space and group \\(G=\\operatorname{SU}(V)\\) of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the circle pushout of the metaplectic double cover.</p><p class=\"li\">For a unitary dual pair with Hermitian dimension \\(m\\) and skew-Hermitian dimension \\(n\\), choose unitary Hecke characters \\(\\chi_H,\\chi_K\\) of \\(E\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\chi_H|_{\\mathbb A_F^\\times/F^\\times}=\\epsilon_{E/F}^{\\,m},\n\\qquad\n\\chi_K|_{\\mathbb A_F^\\times/F^\\times}=\\epsilon_{E/F}^{\\,n}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The unitary splitting restricts to an honest smooth finite-adelic action \\(\\omega_{P,f}\\) of \\(G(\\mathbb A_{F,f})\\), compatible with Lemma 6.2 and independent of the splitting characters. At infinity its half-determinant action is the natural action on \\(L^n\\).</p><p>At a split finite place, with a standard three-dimensional embedding summand as positions for a multiplicity line, the \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3\\)-action is inverse linear substitution. For a transformation \\(h\\) of positions, the normalized linear action is\n\\[\n(\\rho(h)b)(x)=|\\det h|^{-1/2}b(h^{-1}x).\n\\]\nA bare metaplectic lift outside \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3\\) includes its lift scalar, which must be retained.</p><p>Every finite list of inputs is fixed by all sufficiently deep principal lattice congruence subgroups.</p>", "proof": "<p>The required splitting characters exist. The map of idele class groups \\(C_F\\to C_E\\) is injective: an \\(F\\)-idele principal over \\(E\\) has generator fixed by \\(c\\), hence in \\(F\\). It is proper because the norm on \\(C_E\\) restricts to the square of the norm on \\(C_F\\), whose norm-one kernel is compact. Thus \\(C_F\\) is closed in \\(C_E\\), and the character-extension theorem [12, §4.3, Corollary 4.42] extends the required powers of \\(\\epsilon_{E/F}\\).</p><p>Apply Kudla's unitary splitting theorem [19], in the circle-extension and adelic formulation of [5], replacing \\(h_P\\) by the skew-Hermitian form \\(\\delta h_P\\). Its trace product is exactly \\(\\psi_P\\). The global splitting agrees with the canonical rational section on rational unitary points. Changing characters twists by determinant characters, which vanish on \\(G\\). Its finite part is therefore the claimed honest action.</p><p>At the active maximal compact \\(S(U(2)\\times U(1))\\), let \\(\\ell\\) be the character on the negative line. The positive plane has determinant \\(\\ell^{-1}\\), so \\(\\det\\mathcal F_P\\) has character \\(\\ell^{2n}\\). At the other places the determinant character is trivial on \\(\\operatorname{SU}(3)\\). The metaplectic determinant-square-root cover consequently pulls back with square-root character \\(\\ell^n\\).</p><p>A connected real reductive group retracts onto its maximal compact subgroup. Every fundamental-group loop therefore has even symplectic determinant winding, so the lift from the universal cover descends to the double cover. Its half-determinant action is that on \\(L^n\\). The unitary circle lift and this double lift differ by a continuous character of the connected semisimple group \\(G(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb R)\\), hence by the trivial character. Rational compatibility then proves compatibility with Lemma 6.2.</p><p>At a split finite place, positions and the opposite embedding summand are dual stable Lagrangians. The symplectic matrix is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(h^{-t},h)\\), giving the normalized substitution formula up to the bare lift scalar. On \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(F_v)\\), the ratio of the substitution and unitary lifts is a character. It is trivial because \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(F_v)\\) is perfect: elementary matrices generate it and\n\\[\ne_{ij}(u)=[e_{ik}(u),e_{kj}(1)]\n\\]\nfor distinct \\(i,j,k\\).</p><p>Finally, smoothness supplies a common open stabilizer for finitely many inputs. Intersect with the lattice stabilizer. Principal lattice congruence neighborhoods form a basis at the identity, giving the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 4.5, theta one-forms span cotangent fibers", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every finite Schwartz input \\(\\varphi\\), \\(\\Theta_i(\\varphi)\\) is holomorphic on \\(D\\) and descends to every sufficiently deep principal congruence quotient.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/F,V,G,D,\\Lambda,\\mu\\) and a multiplicity line \\(W_i\\) be as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Theta_i(\\varphi)\\) be the \\(c\\mu\\)-projection of \\(T_{1,\\varphi}\\), identified with a one-form using a fixed complex trivialization of the constant line.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every finite Schwartz input \\(\\varphi\\), \\(\\Theta_i(\\varphi)\\) is holomorphic on \\(D\\) and descends to every sufficiently deep principal congruence quotient.</p><p>At any fixed point of \\(D\\), the values \\(\\Theta_i(\\varphi)\\), as \\(\\varphi\\) varies, span the cotangent fiber. Finitely many actual inputs already span it and descend at one common sufficiently deep principal level.</p>", "proof": "<p>Holomorphy and descent follow from Proposition 6.</p><p>Fix \\(x_*\\in D\\), with period point \\(Z\\), and put \\(a=3\\). For \\(0\\neq x_0\\in\\mathcal O_F^a\\), take\n\\[\n\\varphi_k=\\mathbf1_{x_0+k\\widehat{\\mathcal O}_F^{\\,a}}.\n\\]\nIts rational support is exactly \\(x_0+k\\mathcal O_F^a\\), and\n\\[\nT_{1,\\varphi_k}(Z)=\n2\\pi i\\sum_{x\\in x_0+k\\mathcal O_F^a}\n(x_\\tau)_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\pi i\\sum_\\tau x_\\tau^tZ_\\tau x_\\tau\\right).\n\\]\nIn the full Minkowski lattice, let \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) be the minimum norm of a nonzero lattice vector. For \\(m\\neq0\\) and \\(k\\geq2\\|x_0\\|/\\rho\\),\n\\[\n\\|x_0+km\\|\\geq k\\|m\\|/2.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian estimate bounds the tail by a constant times\n\\[\n\\sum_{m\\neq0}\\exp(-ck^2\\|m\\|^2),\n\\]\nwhich tends to zero, uniformly near \\(Z\\). Thus\n\\[\nT_{1,\\varphi_k}(Z)\\longrightarrow\n2\\pi i(x_{0,\\tau})_\\tau\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\pi i\\sum_\\tau x_{0,\\tau}^tZ_\\tau x_{0,\\tau}\\right).\n\\]\nThe exponential is nonzero. The vectors \\((x_{0,\\tau})_\\tau\\) span the real position space, hence complexly span the tensor fiber. The span of actual values is closed because the fiber is finite-dimensional; it contains all these limits and is the full fiber. Projection to the cotangent target is surjective.</p><p>Choose finitely many actual inputs whose values contain a basis, then choose their common principal stabilizer. The sequence of inputs used in the limit need not have a common level.</p><p>On a compact Kähler quotient a holomorphic one-form is closed and harmonic, so a nonzero form has nonzero cohomology class. This proposition does not identify its rational CM source.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 4.6, rank-two tensors, wedges, and fixed-data variation", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At data induced by a rational orthogonal decomposition, \\(\\Xi_W(\\varphi)\\) is holomorphic and, on a sufficiently deep common principal quotient, is a finite sum with constant coefficients of …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(E/F,V,G,D,\\Lambda,\\delta\\) of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W=W_1\\perp W_2\\) be a rational Hermitian plane with line signs \\(d_1,d_2\\), both positive at \\(\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\Xi_W(\\varphi)\\) by projecting \\(T_{2,\\varphi}\\) to\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Sym}^2((\\mathcal F_W)_{c\\mu})\n=\\operatorname{Sym}^2(Q^*\\otimes W_\\mu^*)\n\\]\nand applying \\(\\operatorname{alt}_2\\), with a fixed constant-line trivialization.</p><p class=\"li\">Keep fixed the rational \\(W\\), symplectic space \\((\\mathbb V_W,\\psi_W)\\), rational embedding of \\(G\\), reference rational Schrödinger coordinates, global Weil and splitting choices, finite action \\(\\omega_{W,f}\\), and one total finite input \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At every compact place \\(\\tau\\neq\\mu\\), vary orthogonal decompositions\n\\[\nW_\\tau=W_\\tau^+\\perp W_\\tau^-,\n\\qquad\n\\dim W_\\tau^\\pm=\\#\\{i\\in\\{1,2\\}:d_i(\\tau)=\\pm1\\}.\n\\]\nThe form on \\(W_\\tau^+\\) has sign \\(-\\operatorname{sgn}(t_\\tau)\\), and on \\(W_\\tau^-\\) the opposite sign. Let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the product of these parameter spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">The positive compact pieces are\n\\[\nV_\\tau\\otimes W_\\tau^-,\n\\qquad\nV_\\tau^*\\otimes(W_\\tau^+)^*,\n\\]\nwhere the second multiplicity factor is embedded as \\(\\operatorname{ann}(W_\\tau^-)\\). The active pieces remain exactly \\(L\\otimes W_\\mu\\) and \\(Q^*\\otimes W_\\mu^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t)\\) for the resulting smooth \\((2,0)\\)-form, with locally smooth constant-line trivializations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. At data induced by a rational orthogonal decomposition, \\(\\Xi_W(\\varphi)\\) is holomorphic and, on a sufficiently deep common principal quotient, is a finite sum with constant coefficients of\n\\[\n\\Theta_1(\\varphi_{1,r})\\wedge\\Theta_2(\\varphi_{2,r}).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is an input, product in adapted rational coordinates, for which \\(\\Xi_W(\\varphi)\\neq0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On a fixed torsion-free compact principal quotient \\(X\\) whose level fixes \\(\\varphi\\), the map\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t)\n\\]\nis continuous in the \\(C^\\infty\\) topology. The same finite level works throughout a parameter neighborhood.</p><p>No holomorphy is asserted for an intermediate parameter not induced by rational lines.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a rational orthogonal decomposition, the symplectic space is a rational direct sum. Change to adapted rational coordinates using Proposition 6. The transformed finite Schwartz input is a finite sum\n\\[\n\\sum_r\\varphi_{1,r}\\otimes\\varphi_{2,r}.\n\\]\nIndeed it is invariant under a sufficiently small product compact open subgroup, and only finitely many product cosets meet its support.</p><p>For product inputs, completed theta series multiply. The total degree-two coefficient is\n\\[\nT_2^{(1)}T_0^{(2)}+\nT_1^{(1)}T_1^{(2)}+\nT_0^{(1)}T_2^{(2)}.\n\\]\nThe mixed coefficient is \\(1\\), because of the \\(1/k!\\) normalization. The unmixed terms vanish under double alternation, since both multiplicity factors lie in the same line. The mixed term is the wedge of the projected first tensors. Compatible trivializations make it exactly the displayed wedge; other trivializations introduce a nonzero constant. Rational coordinate covariance has already been accounted for. The ratio of the differential-bundle identifications is invariant under the transitive infinite special-unitary action, hence constant. This proves the finite expansion and holomorphy after double alternation. The full completed second tensor need not be holomorphic. A further common principal level makes all individual inputs descend.</p><p>For nonvanishing, fix a point of \\(D\\). Proposition 7 supplies an input for \\(W_1\\) with nonzero cotangent value and one for \\(W_2\\) with value outside its span. Their wedge is nonzero. Their product input has nonzero double-alternating second tensor, and rational covariance transports it to the reference coordinates.</p><p>For continuity, no rational coordinate change is made as \\(t\\) varies. The compact-place changes occur only in multiplicity factors, so the Lagrangians remain \\(\\operatorname{SU}(3)\\)-invariant with trivial determinant character. The active pieces stay fixed, with determinant character \\(\\ell^4\\), and the half-determinant action remains \\(L^2\\). The same finite stabilizer therefore gives descent for every parameter. There is no finite translation, Hecke choice, or level change.</p><p>Choose smooth nonzero local frames of the constant determinant lines and their square roots. Cover compact \\(X\\) by finitely many charts with precompact lifts to \\(D\\). On their closures times a small compact parameter neighborhood, the period map is smooth with compact image in the Siegel domain, all required derivatives bounded, and imaginary parts uniformly bounded below. The fixed finite input has rational support in one fixed finite union of lattice cosets. The Gaussian estimates of Proposition 6 yield uniform convergence of every fixed order of chart and parameter derivatives. Projection and smooth trivialization preserve these estimates; unchanged equivariance glues them on \\(X\\). This is \\(C^\\infty\\)-continuity, with no cusp estimate needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Corollary 4.7, approximation with fixed total input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are rational orthogonal decompositions \\(W=W_3\\perp W_4\\) with signs \\(d_3,d_4\\), whose total compact-place positive Lagrangians approach those at \\(t_0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Let odd signs \\(d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4\\) satisfy \\(d_1+d_2=d_3+d_4\\) and \\(d_i(\\mu)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Start with \\(W=W_1\\perp W_2\\), compact-place data \\(t_0\\), and a total input \\(\\varphi\\) for which\n\\[\n\\xi=\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t_0)\n\\]\nis a nonzero holomorphic top form on a sufficiently deep compact principal quotient \\(X\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are rational orthogonal decompositions \\(W=W_3\\perp W_4\\) with signs \\(d_3,d_4\\), whose total compact-place positive Lagrangians approach those at \\(t_0\\). The total active positive Lagrangian is exactly unchanged.</p><p>For a sufficiently close rational choice \\(t'\\),\n\\[\n\\xi'=\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t')\n\\]\nis holomorphic on the same quotient \\(X\\), with the same total input, and\n\\[\n\\int_X\\xi\\wedge\\overline{\\xi'}\\neq0.\n\\]\nBoth endpoints admit the wedge expansions of Proposition 8 on a further common principal cover, and their pairing remains nonzero there.</p>", "proof": "<p>At every compact place, equality of the sign sums is equality of the two sign multisets. Reorder the original local lines so the first has the sign required by \\(d_3(\\tau)\\), and choose a nonzero vector on it. After fixing an \\(E\\)-basis of \\(W\\), weak approximation in \\(E\\), coordinate by coordinate at the finitely many complex places, gives one \\(w\\in W(E)\\) arbitrarily close to all selected vectors.</p><p>Nondegeneracy and norm signs are open. For a sufficiently close approximation,\n\\[\nW_3=Ew,\\qquad W_4=W_3^\\perp\n\\]\nis a rational orthogonal decomposition with the prescribed signs. At \\(\\mu\\), the form on \\(W_\\mu\\) is definite, so both signs are automatic.</p><p>Orthogonal projection onto a nondegenerate line is\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto h_W(z,w)h_W(w,w)^{-1}w.\n\\]\nIt varies continuously, so the signed compact-place subspaces and total Lagrangians converge. Reordering only relabels their total positive summands. At the active pair, the pieces are always \\(L\\otimes W_\\mu\\) and \\(Q^*\\otimes W_\\mu^*\\), and remain exactly fixed. Small active norms or large arithmetic denominators do not affect this fixed-total-data comparison.</p><p>Proposition 8 gives smooth convergence\n\\[\n\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t')\\longrightarrow\\Xi_W(\\varphi;t_0)\n\\]\non the same \\(X\\). For \\(\\xi=f\\,dz_1\\wedge dz_2\\), the form \\(\\xi\\wedge\\overline\\xi\\) is a positive multiple of\n\\[\n|f|^2\\,dx_1\\wedge dy_1\\wedge dx_2\\wedge dy_2.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\int_X\\xi\\wedge\\overline\\xi&gt;0\\), and continuity gives the asserted nonzero mixed integral. Endpoint holomorphy follows from rational factorization in Proposition 8, not from holomorphy along the intermediate parameter path.</p><p>Factor the endpoints only afterward. Their finitely many individual inputs have a common principal stabilizer contained in the level of \\(X\\). On the corresponding finite unramified cover \\(\\pi:X'\\to X\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{X'}\\pi^*\\xi\\wedge\\overline{\\pi^*\\xi'}\n=\\deg(\\pi)\\int_X\\xi\\wedge\\overline{\\xi'}\\neq0.\n\\]\nCompactness and torsion-freeness exclude cusp and ramification corrections. Expanding the finite wedge sums selects at least one nonzero mixed period of four individual one-forms.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, projective reference moduli space", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The scheme \\(S\\) is finite type, smooth, and projective over \\(K_0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/F,V,D,\\Lambda,\\mu\\) have the assumptions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For the reference abelian varieties use \\(V\\), not \\(V\\otimes_EP\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe the homology filtration by\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_\\mu=L,\\qquad\n\\mathcal F_c=\\operatorname{ann}(L)\\subset V_\\mu^*,\n\\]\nand at each other conjugate pair fix a full summand on one side and zero on the other.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an imaginary \\(\\delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\in E^\\times\\) making these Lagrangians positive for\n\\[\n\\psi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(x,y)\n=\\operatorname{Tr}_{E/\\mathbb Q}(\\delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}h_V(x,y)),\n\\]\nand scale it to be integral on \\(\\Lambda\\). In this convention \\(-\\psi_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) is the Riemann form.</p><p class=\"li\">The abelian dimension is \\(3s\\), and the fixed Lie ranks are\n\\[\nr_\\mu=2,\\qquad r_c=1,\\qquad\n\\{r_\\tau,r_{c\\tau}\\}=\\{0,3\\}\\quad(\\tau\\notin\\{1,c\\}).\n\\]\nFix the polarization type.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose sufficiently divisible \\(N\\). A full \\(N\\)-marking is an ordered basis of \\(N\\)-torsion, with every pairing matrix allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Over a number field \\(K_0\\subset\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) containing \\(E\\), let \\(S\\) be the reduced scheme of all tuples\n\\[\n(B,\\lambda,\\iota_E,\\eta_N),\n\\]\nwhere \\(B\\) has dimension \\(3s\\), \\(\\lambda\\) has the fixed type, \\(\\iota_E:\\mathcal O_E\\to\\operatorname{End}(B)\\) is unital with Rosati adjoint \\(c\\), \\(\\eta_N\\) is a full marking, and all Lie ranks have the specified values. Retain all connected components.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The scheme \\(S\\) is finite type, smooth, and projective over \\(K_0\\). Its complex connected components are compact ball quotients. For sufficiently divisible \\(N\\), the component arising from \\(\\Lambda\\) is the quotient of \\(D\\) by its principal special-unitary lattice level.</p>", "proof": "<p>The homology filtration is the kernel of the Lie quotient. If \\(J\\) is the complex structure, \\(z=u+iJu\\) lies in that filtration and\n\\[\n\\psi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(z,\\overline z)/i=-2\\psi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(u,Ju).\n\\]\nThis gives the Riemann-form sign and displayed Lie ranks.</p><p>The fine-moduli theorem [31, Chapter 7] gives a quasi-projective scheme of polarized abelian varieties of fixed polarization degree with universal polarization and full ordered torsion marking, without prescribed pairing matrix. After inverting the polarization degree, polarization type is locally constant. Take its open-and-closed part; if needed first take a sufficiently large level prime to that degree and then add the remaining full marking by a finite étale cover.</p><p>Parameterize graphs of finitely many ring generators \\(b\\) of \\(\\mathcal O_E\\) in relative Hilbert schemes. Their graph degrees are bounded. Put \\(\\alpha=1+\\overline b b\\), a nonzero totally positive element of \\(F\\), and choose an integer \\(m&gt;0\\) with \\(m\\alpha^{-1}\\in\\mathcal O_E\\). In every action,\n\\[\n\\deg(\\alpha)\\deg(m\\alpha^{-1})=m^{6s},\n\\]\nso \\(\\deg(\\alpha)\\) is bounded. The normalized Poincaré bundle pulled back by \\((\\mathrm{id},\\lambda)\\) induces \\(2\\lambda\\). The tensor product of its pullbacks to the square restricts on the graph of \\(b\\) to a bundle inducing \\(2\\lambda(1+\\overline b b)\\). Riemann–Roch bounds its Hilbert polynomial, since \\(\\chi(\\mathcal L)^2=\\deg(\\phi_{\\mathcal L})\\). Only finitely many polynomials occur. Impose the homomorphism, ring, Rosati, and Lie-rank identities on these graph loci.</p><p>In characteristic zero the action locus is unramified by rigidity: an infinitesimal difference of homomorphisms would map a proper connected abelian fiber to an additive tangent group and must vanish. It is proper over the polarized base. Over a DVR, each generic generator extends uniquely between the fixed abelian schemes by the Néron mapping property [8]. Ring and Rosati identities extend by uniqueness. The split action idempotents in characteristic zero give locally constant Lie ranks. Thus the valuative criterion applies. Proper, finite-type, unramified implies quasi-finite and hence finite.</p><p>In a marked complex period chart, action matrices are locally constant. They act on the torus exactly when they preserve its homology filtration; the resulting holomorphic endomorphisms are algebraic. Trace duality and Rosati express the alternating form as an imaginary trace multiple of an \\(E/F\\)-Hermitian form on an \\(E\\)-three-space. Isotropy makes conjugate filtrations annihilators. Positivity and the ranks leave one negative active line and constant remaining data. The reduced local locus is a smooth two-ball.</p><p>For a fixed marked polarized \\(E\\)-lattice, every positive filtration in the entire compatible ball yields a polarized abelian variety and the required algebraic action. Conversely every tuple gives such a filtration. Its component is the quotient of the whole ball by the marked lattice stabilizer.</p><p>Every occurring Hermitian space has a definite real place because \\(s\\geq2\\), so it is anisotropic over \\(F\\). Its ball quotient is compact by the arithmetic compactness theorem. The level removes stabilizers. There are finitely many components, since \\(S\\) is finite type. The finite action locus is quasi-projective; its reduced complex space is smooth and compact. It is proper and hence projective. Smoothness and properness descend to \\(K_0\\).</p><p>Finally, the determinant module of \\(\\Lambda\\), even if \\(\\Lambda\\) is not free, is a fractional \\(\\mathcal O_E\\)-ideal. A unitary lattice automorphism has determinant a norm-one unit, all of whose complex conjugates have modulus one, hence a root of unity. A sufficiently divisible principal level kills every nontrivial such determinant. Its stabilizer is precisely the principal special-unitary level.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, good models and ordinary points", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After one finite extension of \\(K\\) and removal of finitely many primes:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be the full reference moduli space of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Enlarge \\(K_0\\) to a number field \\(K\\subset\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) over which the components \\(S_j\\) are geometrically connected and have \\(K\\)-points.</p><p class=\"li\">Use these points as Albanese origins:\n\\[\na_j:S_j\\longrightarrow A_j=\\operatorname{Alb}(S_j),\n\\qquad\nA_S=\\prod_jA_j.\n\\]\nEach \\(A_j\\) is included with zero in the other factors, and\n\\[\nH^1(A_S,\\mathbb Q)\\simeq H^1(S,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After one finite extension of \\(K\\) and removal of finitely many primes:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. the \\(S_j\\) have smooth proper models with geometrically connected fibers;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. the \\(A_j\\) extend to abelian schemes, and the \\(a_j\\) extend;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. the model of \\(S\\) lies in the full tuple parameter scheme, retaining action graphs;</p><p class=\"li num\">4. differences of Albanese images generate every geometric fiber of each \\(A_j\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">5. at every remaining rational prime \\(q\\) completely split in \\(K\\), the ordinary locus of the universal abelian scheme is dense in each geometric component of the special fiber.</p>", "proof": "<p>Spread out the finite collection of schemes, maps, and universal data. Take the closure of \\(S\\) in a projective compactification of the full parameter scheme used in Proposition 10. Its intersection with the boundary has proper closed image in the integer base and misses the generic point. Remove its finitely many primes. Further shrinking gives the asserted smooth proper and abelian models and extended maps.</p><p>For each component, set\n\\[\n\\Delta_j=a_j(S_j)-a_j(S_j)\\subset A_j.\n\\]\nIt is closed, irreducible, symmetric, and contains zero. The nested closed irreducible sum images \\(n\\Delta_j\\) eventually stabilize in dimension. Equality of two successive images makes the stable image invariant under addition by \\(\\Delta_j\\); symmetry makes it the generated abelian subvariety, which is \\(A_j\\) by the Albanese property. Thus, for some \\(n\\),\n\\[\n(S_j\\times S_j)^n\\longrightarrow A_j\n\\]\nis a surjective proper sum map. Spread it out. Its closed image contains the dense generic fiber of the flat abelian scheme, so it is the whole scheme and remains surjective on every geometric fiber.</p><p>To obtain ordinary points, choose in each component's rational Hermitian space an \\(E\\)-line negative at \\(\\mu\\), and diagonalize its orthogonal complement. At the corresponding coordinate negative-line period, the three rational orthogonal projectors split rational homology into three \\(E\\)-lines and preserve the filtration. Integral multiples are endomorphisms.</p><p>These enhanced points are algebraic. Add their endomorphism graphs with the selected Hilbert polynomials to a finite-type parameter scheme over a number field. Rigidity fixes the projector matrices locally. A negative line preserved by all three projectors must be the unique negative coordinate line, so the enhanced point is isolated. An isolated complex point of such a scheme has algebraic coordinates. Enlarge \\(K\\) to define these points and endomorphisms, spread them out, and exclude projector and isogeny denominators.</p><p>At a completely split remaining \\(q\\), the integral projectors and idempotents of \\(\\mathcal O_E\\otimes\\mathbb Z_q\\) split the \\(q\\)-divisible groups into height-one pieces. Heights are the characteristic-zero Tate ranks and remain constant. Over an algebraically closed field a height-one \\(q\\)-divisible group has dimension zero or one; it is respectively étale or multiplicative, using Cartier duality in the second case. Hence each selected reduction is ordinary.</p><p>Ordinariness is open. A smooth geometrically connected fiber is geometrically irreducible, so this nonempty open is dense on every component. Chebotarev supplies infinitely many completely split rational primes after the finite exclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, algebraic Hecke action", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rational spherical Hecke algebra acts in \\( \\operatorname{End}_K(A_S)\\otimes\\mathbb Q \\) and, by pullback, on \\(H^1(S,\\mathbb Q)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(S/K,A_S,a_j\\) and the good models of Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a remaining rational prime \\(q\\) completely split in \\(K\\), prime to the polarization degree, \\(N\\), and the discriminant of \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\iota:\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\hookrightarrow\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_q\\), and normalize \\(\\nu_\\iota(q)=1\\). For \\(\\tau\\in G_E\\), let \\(\\mathfrak p_\\tau\\) be the prime defined by \\(\\iota\\circ\\tau\\), so\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(\\tau(x))=\\operatorname{val}_{\\mathfrak p_\\tau}(x).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Select one block from each conjugate pair, including\n\\[\nw=\\mathfrak p_c,\\qquad\\overline w=\\mathfrak p_1.\n\\]\nThe selected active block has height three and Lie rank one; each other selected block has Lie rank zero or three. Let \\(\\mathcal J\\) denote the selected blocks.</p><p class=\"li\">In chosen bases, use\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_q=\n\\left(\\prod_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\operatorname{GL}_3(\\mathbb Q_q)\\right)\n\\times\\mathbb Q_q^\\times,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal K_q=\n\\left(\\prod_{j\\in\\mathcal J}\\operatorname{GL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\right)\n\\times\\mathbb Z_q^\\times,\n\\]\nwith raw spherical normalization \\(\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal K_q)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A position has multiplier exponent \\(h\\) and elementary exponents \\(a_{j,1},a_{j,2},a_{j,3}\\). The target selected lattice is \\(g_j^{-1}\\Lambda_j\\), with branch representatives modulo the integral subgroup on the left.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational spherical Hecke algebra acts in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{End}_K(A_S)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\]\nand, by pullback, on \\(H^1(S,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p>If\n\\[\nh\\geq0,\\qquad0\\leq a_{j,k}\\leq h,\n\\]\nthe basis operator is an integral homomorphism over \\(K\\). Its pullback on one-forms is the sum of pullbacks over its target branches.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the positive range choose, in each selected block of \\(B[q^h]\\), a subgroup with the specified elementary divisors; in the opposite block take its annihilator. The total kernel \\(H\\) is maximal isotropic, of order \\(q^{3sh}\\). The quotient \\(f:B\\to B'=B/H\\) is required to satisfy\n\\[\nf^\\vee\\lambda'f=q^h\\lambda.\n\\]\nOver characteristic zero, torsion and subgroup choices are finite étale. The free finite-locally-free translation quotient exists as an fppf scheme quotient [36, Tags 07S6–07S7], and is an abelian scheme.</p><p>On complex lattices, if the selected target is \\(\\Lambda_j'\\), its opposite target is\n\\[\nq^{-h}(\\Lambda_j')^\\vee.\n\\]\nThe scaled form is integral and perfect at \\(q\\), unchanged in elementary-divisor valuations elsewhere, and positive. It gives the original polarization type. The field-level isotropic descent criterion [11, Chapter XI, Proposition 11.25] gives the unique fiberwise polarization homomorphism.</p><p>For relative descent, multiplication by \\(q^h\\) descends to \\(g:B'\\to B\\), with \\(gf=[q^h]\\) and \\(fg=[q^h]\\). Put \\(\\beta=g^\\vee\\lambda g\\). Fiberwise \\(\\beta=q^h\\lambda'\\), so \\(\\beta\\) kills \\(B'[q^h]\\). The correspondence base is smooth and reduced, and its torsion is finite étale and reduced, so fiberwise vanishing is relative vanishing. Factor \\(\\beta\\) through the quotient \\([q^h]_{B'}\\). This defines the relative \\(\\lambda'\\), and cancellation gives the displayed identity and uniqueness. This descends the polarization homomorphism, not a chosen line bundle.</p><p>The stable action descends, its Rosati remains \\(c\\), Lie ranks are unchanged, and \\(f\\) transports the full \\(N\\)-basis. Thus the target remains in the full tuple space. Distinct kernels remain distinct branches even when targets are isomorphic.</p><p>For a finite étale source correspondence \\(C\\to S\\), sum its target branches after their Albanese maps to obtain a morphism \\(n_j:S_j\\to A_S\\). Subtract its value at the origin and use the Albanese property. The resulting integral operator satisfies\n\\[\nT(a_j(x)-a_j(y))\n=\n\\sum_{z\\in C_x}a_{j(z)}(t(z))\n-\\sum_{z\\in C_y}a_{j(z)}(t(z)).\n\\]\nConstant translations contribute no differential, so pullback is the raw branch sum.</p><p>Composition has raw spherical convolution multiplicities. Chains of selected overlattices are exactly the spherical lattice chains; the opposite lattices, polarizations, and markings are forced by their composite selected lattices and multiplier. Branch numbers in each target component are locally constant, so origin translations cancel on differences.</p><p>The central position with all selected exponents one and multiplier exponent two is quotient by \\(B[q]\\). Under multiplication by \\(q\\), it has the same polarization and action and marking \\(q\\eta_N\\), a finite-order marking automorphism. Adding a sufficiently large power of this position brings any integer-exponent position into the positive range. Clear that power by the inverse marking automorphism. Positive convolution makes the definition independent of the clearing power. This gives the full rational spherical action, which is commutative by Satake.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 5.5, Frobenius polynomial", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the integral endomorphism ring of \\(\\widetilde A_S\\), \\( \\Phi^3-D_1\\Phi^2+qD_2\\Phi-q^3D_3=0. \\) On degree-one crystalline cohomology, with \\(\\varphi=\\Phi^*\\), the same polynomial holds for \\(\\varphi\\) and the Hecke …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the full moduli space, good split prime \\(q\\), selected active block, and raw Hecke action of Proposition 12.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i=1,2,3\\), let \\(T_i\\) be the full moduli position with \\(i\\) active exponents equal to one, all other selected exponents zero, and multiplier exponent zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_0\\) be central, with multiplier exponent one and active exponents zero. On a nonactive selected block its three exponents are one when that block has Lie rank three, and zero when it has Lie rank zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nD_i=C_0^iT_i.\n\\]\nThese are positive integral operators.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widetilde A_S/\\mathbb F_q\\) be the good reduction at the fixed completion \\(K_v=\\mathbb Q_q\\), and let \\(\\Phi\\) be its \\(q\\)-power Frobenius. Use the same \\(D_i\\) for the reduced operators.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In the integral endomorphism ring of \\(\\widetilde A_S\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi^3-D_1\\Phi^2+qD_2\\Phi-q^3D_3=0.\n\\]\nOn degree-one crystalline cohomology, with \\(\\varphi=\\Phi^*\\), the same polynomial holds for \\(\\varphi\\) and the Hecke pullbacks, not the reciprocal polynomial.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\) and let \\(x\\) be an ordinary point of the special fiber of \\(S\\). Its active \\(q\\)-divisible group splits canonically as\n\\[\nG_w=M_0\\oplus E_0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(M_0\\) is multiplicative of height one and \\(E_0\\) is étale of height two. Each nonactive selected block is wholly multiplicative or étale.</p><p>Define multiset quotient constructions:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X_0\\): active kernel \\(M_0[q]\\), multiplier \\(q\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y_0\\): sum over active kernels \\(\\ell\\subset E_0[q]\\) of dimension one, multiplier \\(q\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Z_0\\): active kernel \\(E_0[q]\\), multiplier \\(q^2\\).</p><p>For multiplier \\(q^h\\), prescribe connected \\(q^h\\)-torsion on every nonactive selected block and the forced annihilator on every opposite block.</p><p>First count specialization multiplicities. Lift \\(x\\) to \\(R=W(k)\\) by smoothness. The \\(q^2\\) special label components of the active \\(q\\)-torsion lift by idempotent lifting. Each has rank \\(q\\); the identity-specializing component is a subgroup \\(H_0\\), and multiplication respects labels. Thus\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow H_0\\longrightarrow G_w[q]\n\\longrightarrow(\\mathbb F_q^2)_R\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nNo splitting over \\(R\\) is asserted.</p><p>After a finite extension splitting the generic torsion, its generic fiber is an \\(\\mathbb F_q\\)-three-space with distinguished line \\(H_{0,\\mathrm{gen}}\\). For a fixed \\(b\\)-dimensional label subspace, subspaces disjoint from this line and mapping isomorphically onto that label are graph lifts, a torsor under a \\(b\\)-dimensional Hom space; there are \\(q^b\\). A subspace containing the distinguished line is the unique full inverse image of its label subspace.</p><p>The schematic closure of a generic subgroup is finite torsion-free over the DVR, hence flat, and remains a subgroup. Its intersection with a label component has its generic rank. A graph has rank one on each label, so its special fiber is the unique reduced point there. Every graph lift over one label subspace therefore specializes to the same étale subgroup. A subspace containing the distinguished line specializes to \\(M_0[q]\\) plus the corresponding étale subgroup.</p><p>The active specialization table is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{operator}&amp;\\text{special active kernel}&amp;\\text{multiplicity}\\\\ \\hline\nD_1&amp;M_0[q]&amp;1\\\\\nD_1&amp;\\ell\\subset E_0[q],\\ \\dim\\ell=1&amp;q\\text{ for each }\\ell\\\\\nD_2&amp;M_0[q]\\oplus\\ell,\\ \\dim\\ell=1&amp;1\\text{ for each }\\ell\\\\\nD_2&amp;E_0[q]&amp;q^2\\\\\nD_3&amp;M_0[q]\\oplus E_0[q]&amp;1 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThere are \\(q+1\\) étale lines, so the first two operator degrees are \\(q^2+q+1\\). Nonactive selected kernels are uniquely their connected \\(q^i\\)-torsion.</p><p>These quotients are full tuples in \\(S\\). Properness extends every generic neighbor to the chosen valuation-ring extension. Apply the Néron mapping property anew to extend its isogeny between the universal abelian schemes. The polarization identity extends. On every fiber the kernel is finite, contained in the kernel of the polarization composite; equal polarization degrees force degree \\(q^{3si}\\). Properness and fiberwise quasi-finiteness make the map finite, and fiberwise flatness of isogenies and the relative flatness criterion make it flat. Its kernel is the closure of the generic kernel.</p><p>Cartier duality commutes with base change, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ann}(H)\n=\\ker(G^D\\to H^D)=(G/H)^D.\n\\]\nHence opposite kernels specialize to the required annihilators. Equal total kernels give canonically isomorphic abelian quotients. Their actions, markings, and descended polarizations are unique, so their full target tuples agree. All targets are ordinary because ordinariness is isogeny invariant.</p><p>The operation \\(X_0\\) is \\(q\\)-power Frobenius on full tuples. Its total kernel is connected \\(q\\)-torsion on every block, including the opposite blocks by polarization duality. It is the kernel of relative Frobenius \\(F_B:B\\to B^{(q)}\\). Frobenius–Verschiebung duality gives\n\\[\nF_B^\\vee\\lambda^{(q)}F_B=[q]\\lambda.\n\\]\nThe action is the twisted action. For the constant marking label group \\(T=(\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z)^{6s}\\),\n\\[\nF_B\\eta_N=\\eta_N^{(q)}F_T.\n\\]\nRelative Frobenius fixes its labels and is an isomorphism on \\(N\\)-torsion, so the marking is exactly the twisted marking, with no multiplication-by-\\(q\\) change. Since the model is over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), this is coordinate-\\(q\\)-power Frobenius, not its inverse.</p><p>Frobenius commutes with \\(Y_0,Z_0\\) as multiset operations, by\n\\[\nF_{B'}f=f^{(q)}F_B.\n\\]\nIt bijectively permutes étale line choices and respects all tuple data. On the active block, \\(X_0Y_0\\) has kernel \\(M_0[q]\\oplus\\ell\\), multiplier \\(q^2\\), and \\(X_0Z_0\\) has kernel \\(M_0[q]\\oplus E_0[q]\\), multiplier \\(q^3\\).</p><p>The opposite kernels of these composites are forced. For a composite with multiplier \\(q^h\\), its polarization adjoint on \\(q\\)-divisible groups satisfies\n\\[\nf^\\dagger f=[q^h],\\qquad ff^\\dagger=[q^h].\n\\]\nAs fppf subgroup sheaves,\n\\[\n\\ker f=f^\\dagger(G'[q^h]).\n\\]\nAdjunction makes this image isotropic. In each conjugate pair,\n\\[\nK_{\\overline j}\\subset K_j^\\perp,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}(K_j)\\operatorname{rank}(K_{\\overline j})\\leq q^{3h}.\n\\]\nCartier duality gives equal ranks for \\(\\ker f\\) and \\(\\ker f^\\dagger\\); their composite has kernel rank \\(q^{6sh}\\), so \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\ker f=q^{3sh}\\). The product of the inequalities is an equality, forcing equality in each pair. Thus \\(K_{\\overline j}=K_j^\\perp\\). Nonactive connected kernels compose as prescribed. Uniqueness identifies all composite targets.</p><p>The resulting multiset identities are\n\\[\nD_1:X_0+qY_0,\\qquad\nD_2:X_0Y_0+q^2Z_0,\\qquad\nD_3:X_0Z_0.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal O\\) be all ordinary \\(k\\)-points and define\n\\[\n\\alpha:\\mathbb Z[\\mathcal O]\\longrightarrow\\widetilde A_S(k),\n\\qquad\n[x]\\longmapsto a_{j(x)}(x).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\Phi\\alpha=\\alpha X_0\\). Specializing the integral Albanese difference formula of Proposition 12 gives\n\\[\nD_i(a_j(x)-a_j(y))=\\alpha(N_i([x]-[y])),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nN_1=X_0+qY_0,\\quad N_2=X_0Y_0+q^2Z_0,\\quad N_3=X_0Z_0.\n\\]\nThese equalities retain actual geometric-point groups and branch multiplicities; no rationalization of finite-field point groups is used.</p><p>Frobenius preserves each component. The desired endomorphism on a difference is therefore \\(\\alpha\\) applied to\n\\[\n(X_0^3-N_1X_0^2+qN_2X_0-q^3N_3)([x]-[y]).\n\\]\nThe operator vanishes because\n\\[\n-qY_0X_0^2+qX_0Y_0X_0+q^3Z_0X_0-q^3X_0Z_0=0.\n\\]\nOnly commutation with \\(X_0\\) is used. Ordinary density and the fiberwise finite-sum Albanese generation in Proposition 11 force the endomorphism to vanish on every Albanese factor, hence on \\(\\widetilde A_S\\). Faithful base change gives the identity over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\).</p><p>Crystalline Frobenius is pullback by this \\(q\\)-power endomorphism; Witt Frobenius on \\(W(\\mathbb F_q)=\\mathbb Z_q\\) is the identity because \\(q\\) is prime. Pullback is additive on abelian \\(H^1\\). It reverses composition, but these operators commute, giving the same polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 6.3, active spherical parameter", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every occurring active character has projective parameter \\( \\{q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e\\}, \\) where \\(e=e_0^{-1}\\) for a root \\(e_0^M=\\omega\\) supplied by Lemma 14.2.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/F,V,D\\) be as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd with \\(v(1)=1\\), and use a multiplicity line with signs \\(d(\\tau)=v(\\tau^{-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite collection of theta inputs and their principal level before choosing the good split prime \\(q\\) of Propositions 11–12. Exclude the finitely many exceptional local data of those inputs.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H_w^0\\) be the rational spherical subalgebra whose multiplier exponent is zero, whose nonactive selected blocks are trivial, and whose active exponent sum is zero. It is the spherical algebra of \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Q_q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An active character occurs in a theta class if the corresponding simultaneous generalized eigenspace in its finite-dimensional Hecke span has nonzero projection of that class.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the half-modulus Satake convention attached to normalized parabolic induction, without Gross's additional central Hecke/Tate shift. A projective \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3\\)-parameter is an unordered nonzero triple modulo common scalar.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every occurring active character has projective parameter\n\\[\n\\{q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(e=e_0^{-1}\\) for a root \\(e_0^M=\\omega\\) supplied by Lemma 14.2. The number \\(e\\) is algebraic, all its complex conjugates have modulus one, and\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma e)\n=-\\frac32d((\\sigma|_E)^{-1})\n=-\\frac32v(\\sigma|_E)\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}/\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p>A scalar-conjugate active character has a representative of the displayed form with third entry \\(e'=\\pm\\sigma e\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At the good split prime, separate finite inputs into local tensors and average over \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\). This yields radial active factors. The group is transitive on vectors with fixed minimum coordinate valuation, so invariant Schwartz functions are finite linear combinations of the linearly independent \\(b_j\\). They form a cyclic Laurent module for \\(D_q\\).</p><p>The active spherical action commutes with \\(D_q\\). Lemma 14.2 quotients the Laurent module by \\(D_q^M-\\omega\\), which splits into one-dimensional spaces \\(D_q=e_0\\), since \\(X^M-\\omega\\) has distinct nonzero roots. This concerns the radial quotient only, not semisimplicity of cohomology.</p><p>For the local computation extend substitutions to\n\\[\n\\rho(g)b(x)=|\\det g|_q^{-1/2}b(g^{-1}x),\n\\qquad g\\in\\operatorname{GL}_3(\\mathbb Q_q).\n\\]\nThis is computational, not theta equivariance for the full similitude group. Let \\(K_3=\\operatorname{GL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\), and let \\(T_i\\) be the raw minuscule coset with \\(i\\) diagonal entries \\(q\\). Right cosets correspond to sublattices between \\(q\\mathbb Z_q^3\\) and \\(\\mathbb Z_q^3\\) of index \\(q^i\\). For \\(i=1,2\\), there are \\(q^2+q+1\\); the numbers containing a fixed primitive vector are \\(q+1\\) and \\(1\\). Thus\n\\[\nT_1b_0=q^{1/2}\\bigl((q+1)b_0+q^2b_1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_2b_0=q\\bigl(b_0+(q^2+q)b_1\\bigr),\n\\qquad T_3b_0=D_qb_0.\n\\]\nOn \\(D_q=e_0\\), \\(b_1=q^{-3/2}e_0b_0\\), giving character values\n\\[\nq e_1(e_0,q^{1/2},q^{-1/2}),\\quad\nq e_2(e_0,q^{1/2},q^{-1/2}),\\quad\ne_3(e_0,q^{1/2},q^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThe half-modulus Satake transforms are \\(qe_1,qe_2,e_3\\) [14]. Hence the parameter is \\(\\{e_0,q^{1/2},q^{-1/2}\\}\\).</p><p>Lemma 14.1 inverts it. Inversion introduces no degree factor: \\(T_1^\\vee=T_3^{-1}T_2\\), \\(T_2^\\vee=T_3^{-1}T_1\\), \\(T_3^\\vee=T_3^{-1}\\), and Satake sends these to the reciprocal triple. Therefore the geometric projective parameter is \\(\\{q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e_0^{-1}\\}\\).</p><p>The determinant-zero \\(\\operatorname{GL}_3\\) and \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3\\) cosets and their Satake integrals agree. The half-sum of positive roots is \\((1,0,-1)\\), integral on the relevant cocharacter lattices, so the comparison is rational [14, Proposition 3.6]. Scalar conjugation conjugates the projective parameter. It may change both \\(q^{1/2}\\) and \\(q^{-1/2}\\) by a common sign; removing that sign leaves \\(e'=\\pm\\sigma e\\).</p><p>For the valuation, set \\(g=\\sigma|_E\\). Then\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma\\tau(r))=\\operatorname{val}_{\\mathfrak p_{g\\tau}}(r).\n\\]\nOnly \\(g\\tau=1,c\\) contributes. Among \\(\\Sigma\\), the contributing representative is \\(g^{-1}\\), with valuation \\(-M\\), or \\(cg^{-1}\\), with valuation \\(M\\). Oddness and centrality of \\(c\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}d(\\tau)\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma\\tau(r))\n=-Md(g^{-1}).\n\\]\nTaking valuations in Lemma 14.2 and \\(e_0^M=\\omega\\) yields\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma e_0)=\\frac32d(g^{-1}),\n\\]\nand inversion gives the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every double coset in \\(\\mathcal H_w^0\\) has all branches within each component of \\(S\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the moduli and active spherical algebra of Proposition 14.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every double coset in \\(\\mathcal H_w^0\\) has all branches within each component of \\(S\\). On the original ball component, its pullback action on theta classes is the inverse spherical action on the active local Weil input: a local double coset acts through its inverse, with raw multiplicities and no degree normalization.</p>", "proof": "<p>Such a quasi-isogeny fixes the rational polarized period and changes only the active lattice and its opposite dual, retaining marking and all other local data. The description agrees with central clearing in Proposition 12 after undoing its marking change. A determinant-exponent-zero position has a representative in \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Q_q)\\). Adjusting determinant units in Smith bases identifies its branches with the \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\) double-coset branches.</p><p>For each component, apply strong approximation to its rational special-unitary group. It is simply connected and absolutely almost simple over \\(F\\), with noncompact factor at \\(\\mu\\), so its rational points are dense in the finite adelic points [27, Theorem 4.16].</p><p>For a changed lattice \\(g^{-1}\\Lambda_w^{(j)}\\), choose a rational \\(\\gamma\\) with active component in \\(K_qg\\), where \\(K_q=\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\), and all other finite components integral and marking-preserving. Then \\(\\gamma\\) returns the full changed lattice to \\(\\Lambda^{(j)}\\), identifying the target with a transformed negative period line in the same component.</p><p>On the original component, Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\n\\gamma^*\\Theta(\\omega_f(\\gamma)\\varphi)=\\Theta(\\varphi).\n\\]\nThus fixed-input pullback acts by \\(\\omega_f(\\gamma)^{-1}\\). Other finite components fix the input; the active input is \\(K_q\\)-invariant at the chosen good prime. If \\(\\gamma_w=kg\\), the action is \\(\\omega_f(g^{-1})\\). Summing branches proves the raw inverse action.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(M>0\\), \\(r\\in E^\\times\\), and \\(\\omega\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}^{\\times}\\) with \\( r\\overline r=1,\\qquad \\operatorname{val}_w(r)=M,\\qquad \\operatorname{val}_{\\overline w}(r)=-M, \\) and zero valuation …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the multiplicity-line theta construction of Proposition 14.</p><p class=\"li\">At the split place \\(w\\), take the \\(w\\)-embedding three-space as Schrödinger positions, with lattice \\(\\mathbb Z_q^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Z_q)\\)-invariant Schwartz functions are radial and span\n\\[\nb_j=\\mathbf1_{q^j\\mathbb Z_q^3},\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n(D_qb)(x)=q^{3/2}b(q^{-1}x).\n\\]\nThen \\(D_qb_j=q^{3/2}b_{j+1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the input at all finite places other than \\(w\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(M&gt;0\\), \\(r\\in E^\\times\\), and \\(\\omega\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}^{\\times}\\) with\n\\[\nr\\overline r=1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{val}_w(r)=M,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{val}_{\\overline w}(r)=-M,\n\\]\nand zero valuation elsewhere, such that the radial-input map to theta one-form classes factors through \\(D_q^M=\\omega\\).</p><p>In the covariance convention of Proposition 6,\n\\[\n\\omega^2=\\zeta\\prod_{\\tau\\in\\Sigma}\\tau(r)^{-3d(\\tau)}\n\\]\nfor a root of unity \\(\\zeta\\). Every root of \\(X^M-\\omega\\) has modulus one under every complex embedding.</p>", "proof": "<p>Principalize \\(w^m=(a)\\) and put \\(r=a/\\overline a\\). It has norm one and the required valuations with \\(M=m\\). Elsewhere it is a unit. At finitely many exceptional places, replace \\(r\\) by a common positive power entering the open stabilizers of the fixed inputs in the bare local Weil representation. Powers enter prescribed open subgroups of compact unit groups because their finite quotients have finite order. Replace \\(M\\) accordingly.</p><p>Scalar multiplication by \\(r\\) on \\(V\\otimes_EP\\) is symplectic. Use its bare rational covariance with paired metaplectic lifts, not an arbitrary unitary splitting character. At \\(w\\), normalized linear substitution gives \\(D_q^M\\) up to a root of unity; the scalar unit part fixes radial functions. Almost everywhere else the input is unramified and \\(r\\) integral. At exceptional places the chosen power fixes the input under a suitable bare lift; the paired lift differs by its finite Weil phase. Their product is a root of unity. An arbitrary circle lift would not justify this conclusion.</p><p>At infinity, \\(r\\) acts by \\(\\tau(r)\\) on the \\(\\tau\\)-summand and \\(\\tau(r)^{-1}\\) on the conjugate summand. It fixes the period and positive Lagrangian. The square of the scalar on the projected first tensor is the determinant scalar times the square of the projected \\(c\\mu\\)-scalar.</p><p>At \\(\\mu\\), the filtration exponents are \\(1,-1,-1\\): its determinant exponent is \\(-1\\), and the squared projected factor contributes \\(-2\\), giving \\(-3\\). At a compact place the chosen full rank-three summand contributes \\(-3d(\\tau)\\). This includes the constant determinant line, trivial only on \\(\\operatorname{SU}(V)\\).</p><p>The covariance formula has the transformed finite input on its left. It therefore gives\n\\[\n\\Theta(D_q^Mb)=\\omega\\Theta(b)\n\\]\nwith the stated square. The right side is algebraic. Every complex conjugate of \\(r\\) has modulus one because \\(E\\) is CM and \\(r\\overline r=1\\); hence every conjugate of \\(\\omega\\), and every root of \\(X^M-\\omega\\), has modulus one. For a finite separated sum of inputs choose common stabilizers; the same \\(r,M,\\omega\\) works.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 6.6, from Frobenius slopes to Hodge types", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every full character \\(\\chi\\) extending \\(\\eta\\), the space \\(H_\\chi\\otimes_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\mathbb C\\) is contained in \\(H^{1,0}(A_S)\\) if \\(t_\\eta=1\\), and in \\(H^{0,1}(A_S)\\) if \\(t_\\eta=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(S/K,A_S,q,\\iota\\), the full raw Hecke action, and \\(D_i=C_0^iT_i\\) of Propositions 12–14.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb T\\) be the image of the full spherical algebra in \\(\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb Q}H^1(A_S,\\mathbb Q)\\). It is finite-dimensional and commutative.</p><p class=\"li\">A numerical character is a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-algebra map \\(\\chi:\\mathbb T\\to\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\). After extending coefficients, the product idempotents \\(p_\\chi\\) of its local Artinian factors define\n\\[\nH_\\chi=p_\\chi H^1(A_S,\\overline{\\mathbb Q}).\n\\]\nFor an active character \\(\\eta\\), put\n\\[\nH_\\eta^{\\mathrm{act}}=\\bigoplus_{\\chi|_{\\mathrm{act}}=\\eta}H_\\chi.\n\\]\nAll generalized and nilpotent parts are retained.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta\\) have the projective parameter\n\\[\n\\{q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e'\\}\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 14 or a scalar conjugate. Thus all complex conjugates of \\(e'\\) have modulus one and \\(\\nu_\\iota(e')\\in\\{-3/2,3/2\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nt_\\eta=\\frac12-\\frac13\\nu_\\iota(e')\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every full character \\(\\chi\\) extending \\(\\eta\\), the space \\(H_\\chi\\otimes_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\mathbb C\\) is contained in \\(H^{1,0}(A_S)\\) if \\(t_\\eta=1\\), and in \\(H^{0,1}(A_S)\\) if \\(t_\\eta=0\\). Consequently the entire active generalized space has that pure Hodge type.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Hecke maps are pullbacks of rational endomorphisms over \\(K\\). On abelian \\(H^1\\), pullback is additive in homomorphisms: addition pulls back with identity components under Künneth. Betti pullback is faithful by Proposition 1. Hence all polynomial relations in \\(\\mathbb T\\) also hold among endomorphisms and in de Rham and crystalline degree-one realizations. The algebraic-coefficient projectors therefore remain idempotents there and preserve filtration. No semisimplicity is assumed.</p><p>Fix a full \\(\\chi\\) extending \\(\\eta\\). Its active \\(\\operatorname{GL}_3\\)-parameter is a scalar multiple of the projective triple. Absorb \\(\\chi(C_0)\\) into the scalar and write\n\\[\n(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2,\\alpha_3)=\nb(q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e').\n\\]\nLemma 15.1 implies\n\\[\nb^3e'\\text{ is a root of unity}.\n\\]\nThus \\(b\\) is algebraic and every complex conjugate has modulus one. Raw Satake gives\n\\[\n\\chi(D_1)=qe_1(\\alpha),\\qquad\n\\chi(D_2)=qe_2(\\alpha),\\qquad\n\\chi(D_3)=e_3(\\alpha).\n\\]</p><p>On\n\\[\nD_S=H_{\\mathrm{dR}}^1((A_S)_{K_v}/\\mathbb Q_q),\n\\]\nProposition 13 gives\n\\[\n\\varphi^3-D_1^*\\varphi^2+qD_2^*\\varphi-q^3D_3^*=0.\n\\]\nThe extending Hecke maps commute with \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p>For \\(\\ell\\neq q\\), Weil's theorem gives\n\\[\nP_\\Phi(X)=\\det(X-\\Phi\\mid V_\\ell\\widetilde A_S)\\in\\mathbb Z[X],\n\\]\nwith all complex roots of absolute value \\(q^{1/2}\\). Faithfulness of the Tate-module action and Cayley–Hamilton give \\(P_\\Phi(\\Phi)=0\\) in the endomorphism ring, hence \\(P_\\Phi(\\varphi)=0\\) on \\(H^1\\). This applies to arbitrary abelian varieties over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), without CM or ordinary assumptions [24].</p><p>Transport \\(p_\\chi\\) and its coefficients using \\(\\iota\\). Simultaneous triangularization of the commuting Hecke maps and \\(\\varphi\\) implies every diagonal Frobenius eigenvalue is a root of\n\\[\nX^3-qe_1(\\alpha)X^2+q^2e_2(\\alpha)X-q^3e_3(\\alpha)\n=\\prod_{j=1}^3(X-q\\alpha_j).\n\\]\nThe candidates before applying \\(\\iota\\) are\n\\[\nbq^{3/2},\\qquad bq^{1/2},\\qquad qbe'.\n\\]\nIf the image of one is a root of the rational \\(P_\\Phi\\), the algebraic number itself is a root. Their complex absolute values are respectively \\(q^{3/2},q^{1/2},q\\), so only \\(bq^{1/2}\\) remains.</p><p>Every eigenvalue on the whole generalized summand therefore has slope\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(bq^{1/2})\n=\\frac12-\\frac13\\nu_\\iota(e')=t_\\eta.\n\\]\nJordan blocks do not change this. Choose a finite local coefficient field containing the projector and character coefficients. Lemma 15.2 makes the projected filtration pure of index \\(t_\\eta\\).</p><p>To compare with the same complex Betti space, put\n\\[\nV_{\\mathrm{dR}}=H_{\\mathrm{dR}}^1(A_S/K).\n\\]\nFor the actual compositum \\(k_\\chi\\subset\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) of \\(K\\) and the coefficients of a polynomial expression for \\(p_\\chi\\), its image and projected filtration have ranks of matrices over \\(k_\\chi\\). These ranks are unchanged under \\(\\iota|_{k_\\chi}\\). Betti–de Rham comparison over the given \\(K\\subset\\mathbb C\\) identifies them with the ranks on \\(H_\\chi\\otimes\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>For a scalar-conjugate character, repeat separately with \\(p_{\\sigma\\chi}\\) in the same \\(K\\)-defined operators. Their rational Betti matrices give \\(\\sigma H_\\chi=H_{\\sigma\\chi}\\). The actual-compositum argument remains valid even when \\(K\\) meets the character field.</p><p>Complex Hecke maps preserve both Hodge types. Thus full projected \\(F^1\\) means wholly holomorphic, and zero projected \\(F^1\\) means wholly antiholomorphic. The slope depends only on \\(\\eta\\), so summing all full extensions proves the active-space assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Both \\(T_3\\) and \\(C_0\\) have finite order on the full tuple space \\(S\\) and on \\(A_S\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_3,C_0\\) be the central full-moduli positions specified in Proposition 13.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(T_3\\) and \\(C_0\\) have finite order on the full tuple space \\(S\\) and on \\(A_S\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each central position has a unique lattice choice. For \\(T_3\\), take a norm-one \\(r\\in E^\\times\\) with valuations \\(M,-M\\) at \\(w,\\overline w\\) and zero elsewhere, as in Lemma 14.2. Scalar multiplication by \\(r\\) returns the \\(T_3^M\\)-target lattice to the source. It preserves every polarized period. Its denominators are supported at \\(q\\); a further power fixes the \\(N\\)-marking. That power acts identically on every tuple.</p><p>For \\(C_0\\), let \\(I\\) be the product of the primes whose full exponent is one. It contains one prime from each conjugate pair, including \\(\\overline w\\), and \\(I\\overline I=(q)\\). Choose \\(I^m=(a)\\). Then\n\\[\na\\overline a=q^mu\n\\]\nfor a totally positive unit \\(u\\in\\mathcal O_F^\\times\\). The scalar \\(b=a^2/u\\) has the valuations of \\(I^{2m}\\) and satisfies \\(b\\overline b=q^{2m}\\). It identifies the powered target and source with exactly the required polarization multiplier. A further power fixes the marking. The scalar argument works on every Hermitian component because it preserves every embedding filtration. The induced Albanese maps also have finite order.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The filtered Frobenius module \\(D_A\\) is weakly admissible, its filtration has only indices \\(0,1\\), and extending endomorphisms preserve its filtration and commute with Frobenius.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A/\\mathbb Z_q\\) be an abelian scheme, \\(A\\) its generic fiber, and\n\\[\nD_A=H_{\\mathrm{dR}}^1(A/\\mathbb Q_q)\n\\]\nwith Hodge filtration and good-reduction crystalline Frobenius \\(\\varphi_A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L/\\mathbb Q_q\\) be finite.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be an \\(L\\)-linear idempotent on \\(D_A\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q_q}L\\) preserving the filtration and commuting with \\(\\varphi_A\\otimes1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The filtered Frobenius module \\(D_A\\) is weakly admissible, its filtration has only indices \\(0,1\\), and extending endomorphisms preserve its filtration and commute with Frobenius.</p><p>If every Frobenius slope on the image of \\(p\\) equals \\(t\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), then\n\\[\nF^1p(D_A\\otimes L)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;t=0,\\\\\np(D_A\\otimes L),&amp;t=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Good-reduction crystalline comparison identifies\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{cris}}\\!\\left(\nH_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}^1(A_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_q},\\mathbb Q_q)\n\\right)\n\\simeq\nH_{\\mathrm{cris}}^1(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb F_q}/\\mathbb Z_q)[1/q]\n\\]\nand identifies the filtered de Rham realization with \\(D_A\\), functorially for extending endomorphisms [4, Theorems 1.1(i), 12.1, and §12.2]. The paper invokes the formal-model and algebraic/analytic comparisons [7, §7.2; 34, Theorems 9.1 and 9.3, with 35], passage to completion [36, Tag 0DDG], and inverse limits from \\(\\mathbb Z/q^n\\)-coefficients. Frobenius is special-fiber \\(q\\)-power pullback because Witt Frobenius on \\(\\mathbb Z_q\\) is the identity. Crystalline representations give weak admissibility [9, Theorem 9.3.4].</p><p>Extension by \\(L\\) changes coefficients, not the geometric base. With trivial Frobenius on \\(L\\), the underlying \\(\\mathbb Q_q\\)-filtered module is a direct sum of \\([L:\\mathbb Q_q]\\) copies of \\(D_A\\), hence weakly admissible.</p><p>Both \\(p\\) and \\(1-p\\) preserve filtration and Frobenius. Weak admissibility gives \\(t_H\\leq t_N\\) on each image. Additivity and equality for the whole module force equality on each image. If\n\\[\nm=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q_q}p(D_A\\otimes L),\n\\]\npure slope \\(t\\) gives \\(t_N=tm\\), including after restriction of scalars: all local conjugates have the same valuation, even for ramified \\(L\\). Since the filtration has only indices zero and one,\n\\[\nt_H=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q_q}F^1p(D_A\\otimes L).\n\\]\nEquality gives the result.</p><p>Preservation by the projector is essential. The paper's example\n\\[\n\\varphi=\\operatorname{diag}(1,q),\\qquad\nF^1=\\langle e_0+e_1\\rangle\n\\]\nis weakly admissible, but its slope-one line has induced \\(F^1=0\\); its slope projector does not preserve the filtration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Theorem 5.1, degree-one CM extraction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a common sufficiently deep principal ball quotient \\(S_0=\\Gamma\\backslash D\\), a smooth connected projective surface, on which every associated theta one-form class belongs to …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/F,V,D,\\Lambda\\) have the assumptions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd with \\(v(1)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the multiplicity-line construction with signs \\(d(\\tau)=v(\\tau^{-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U(v)=H^1(B(v),\\mathbb Q)\\), as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite collection of finite adelic Schwartz inputs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a common sufficiently deep principal ball quotient \\(S_0=\\Gamma\\backslash D\\), a smooth connected projective surface, on which every associated theta one-form class belongs to\n\\[\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\n\\left\\{\nf(U(v)_1):\nf\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(U(v),H^1(S_0,\\mathbb Q))\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe maps are rational Hodge maps, and their images are extended to \\(\\mathbb C\\). The conclusion persists at every deeper principal level.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the common principal level and projective reference moduli space by Propositions 6 and 10, then choose the good split prime after fixing all inputs and levels, using Proposition 11. Identify\n\\[\nH^1(A_S,\\mathbb Q)\\simeq H^1(S,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nExtend a theta class from \\(S_0\\) by zero on the other components. Lemma 14.1 makes this compatible with the active algebra.</p><p>Decompose the class into active generalized spaces. For a nonzero component with character \\(\\eta\\) and parameter \\(e\\), Propositions 14–15 give, for every scalar automorphism \\(\\sigma\\), with \\(g=\\sigma|_E\\),\n\\[\n\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma e)=-\\frac32v(g),\\qquad\nt_{\\sigma\\eta}\n=\\frac12-\\frac13\\nu_\\iota(\\sigma e)\n=\\frac{1+v(g)}2.\n\\]\nThe possible sign in \\(\\pm\\sigma e\\) has valuation zero. The entire conjugate active space is therefore holomorphic if \\(v(g)=1\\), antiholomorphic otherwise. At the identity this agrees with the theta class's holomorphy.</p><p>Each active space is defined over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) in rational Betti cohomology. For a nonzero algebraic-Betti vector \\(x\\in H_\\eta^{\\mathrm{act}}\\) and \\(0\\neq u\\in U(v)_1(\\overline{\\mathbb Q})\\), define the \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-linear map\n\\[\nh:U(v)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\longrightarrow H^1(S,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})\n\\]\nby \\(h(u)=x\\) and zero on all other embedding lines.</p><p>Scalar conjugation moves the nonzero source line to \\(U(v)_g\\), and the target to \\(\\sigma x\\in H_{\\sigma\\eta}^{\\mathrm{act}}\\). Their types agree by the displayed formula. Thus every scalar conjugate of \\(h\\) is a Hodge map.</p><p>Lemma 1.1 expresses \\(h\\) as an algebraic-coefficient linear combination of rational Hodge maps. Algebraic-Betti vectors span each complex active space, so complex spans include every original theta component, including those with transcendental coefficients. Restriction to \\(S_0\\) gives the assertion.</p><p>For finitely many inputs all level choices precede the prime choice and exclude only finitely many primes, so the argument applies simultaneously. A deeper principal covering has rational Hodge pullback and pulls the theta forms to their corresponding classes. Pulling back the span identity proves persistence. By Proposition 1, each rational Hodge map in the span is, up to a rational multiple, pullback by a morphism \\(S_0\\to B(v)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Corollary 6.7, finite-family CM extraction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a common deeper principal cover \\(Y\\to X_{\\mathrm{base}}\\), with \\(Y\\) smooth, connected, and projective, on which every theta class in the \\(j\\)-th family belongs to …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(E/F,V,D,\\Lambda\\) and principal lattice levels as in Proposition 16.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd with \\(v_j(1)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(j\\), choose finitely many inputs in the multiplicity-line construction with \\(d_j(\\tau)=v_j(\\tau^{-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_{\\mathrm{base}}\\) be a torsion-free principal quotient in this framework.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a common deeper principal cover \\(Y\\to X_{\\mathrm{base}}\\), with \\(Y\\) smooth, connected, and projective, on which every theta class in the \\(j\\)-th family belongs to\n\\[\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\n\\left\\{\nf(U(v_j)_1):\nf\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(U(v_j),H^1(Y,\\mathbb Q))\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThis persists under every deeper principal pullback. Auxiliary primes for different families may differ.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 16 to each finite family. Choose a principal level contained in all the resulting levels and the base level, and deepen it until Proposition 10 applies. The quotient is smooth, connected, and projective. The reference moduli construction depends only on \\(V\\), not on the multiplicity line. Persistence in Proposition 16 pulls all span identities to \\(Y\\) and deeper levels. Each application chooses its auxiliary prime after its own family and level are fixed; the primes need not agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Theorem 2.5, four-factor switch", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the Künneth inclusion, the line \\( U(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c \\subset H^4\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4B(v_i),\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\right) \\) is algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be a Galois CM field, \\(c\\) its conjugation, and \\(G_E=\\operatorname{Gal}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd sign functions satisfying\n\\[\nv_1+v_2=v_3+v_4.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the CM abelian varieties \\(B(v_i)\\) of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under the Künneth inclusion, the line\n\\[\nU(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c\n\\subset\nH^4\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4B(v_i),\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\right)\n\\]\nis algebraic.</p><p>Its scalar conjugates already have Hodge type \\((2,2)\\), since their type-sign sum is\n\\[\nv_1(g)+v_2(g)-v_3(g)-v_4(g)=0\n\\qquad(g\\in G_E).\n\\]\nThe assertion is algebraicity, not merely this balance.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 3, treat \\(s\\geq2\\) and \\(v_i(1)=1\\). Put\n\\[\nd_i(\\tau)=v_i(\\tau^{-1}).\n\\]\nThese functions retain the relation and are positive at \\(\\mu=1\\).</p><p>Choose \\(V,D\\) and \\(W=W_1\\perp W_2\\) for \\(d_1,d_2\\) as in Proposition 6. Proposition 8 supplies a total input giving a nonzero top form. With this input fixed, Proposition 9 gives a second rational orthogonal decomposition and a nonzero mixed period.</p><p>On its individual-factor principal cover \\(X'\\), write the endpoint forms as\n\\[\n\\xi_1=\\sum_{a\\in I}c_a\\Theta_1(\\varphi_{1a})\\wedge\\Theta_2(\\varphi_{2a}),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\xi_2=\\sum_{b\\in J}c'_b\\Theta_3(\\varphi_{3b})\\wedge\\Theta_4(\\varphi_{4b}),\n\\qquad\n\\int_{X'}\\xi_1\\wedge\\overline{\\xi_2}\\neq0.\n\\]\nThe constants record constant-line trivializations. All input families are finite, and every deeper covering multiplies the integral by its degree.</p><p>Apply Proposition 17 to these four families, with base \\(X'\\), obtaining one smooth connected projective principal cover \\(\\pi:Y\\to X'\\). Write\n\\[\n\\vartheta_{ia}=[\\pi^*\\Theta_i(\\varphi_{ia})]\\in H^1(Y,\\mathbb C).\n\\]\nThe covering formula and closed holomorphic representatives give\n\\[\n0\\neq\n\\int_Y\\pi^*\\xi_1\\wedge\\overline{\\pi^*\\xi_2}\n=\n\\sum_{a\\in I,b\\in J}\nc_a\\overline{c'_b}\n\\left\\langle\n\\vartheta_{1a}\\smile\\vartheta_{2a}\n\\smile\\overline{\\vartheta_{3b}}\\smile\\overline{\\vartheta_{4b}},\n[Y]\\right\\rangle.\n\\]\nChoose a nonzero summand and denote its four holomorphic classes by \\(\\vartheta_i\\).</p><p>Proposition 17 places each \\(\\vartheta_i\\) in the rational-Hodge-image span of \\(U(v_i)_1\\). Rational maps commute with scalar complex conjugation, which sends \\(U(v_i)_1\\) to \\(U(v_i)_c\\). Therefore \\(\\overline{\\vartheta_i}\\), for \\(i=3,4\\), lies in the corresponding span of \\(U(v_i)_c\\).</p><p>The four classes\n\\[\n\\vartheta_1,\\quad\\vartheta_2,\\quad\n\\overline{\\vartheta_3},\\quad\\overline{\\vartheta_4}\n\\]\nsatisfy Proposition 5 on the same surface \\(Y\\), with labels \\((1,1,c,c)\\). The normalized line is algebraic. Proposition 3 returns every switch to this case or to ordinary algebraic pairs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, effective realization of a simple CM Hodge structure", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One has \\(n\\geq0\\), and there is a CM abelian variety \\(B\\), possibly a point, and a surjective rational Hodge map \\( H^n(B,\\mathbb Q)(-r_0)\\longrightarrow W. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A polarizable rational Hodge structure is of CM type when the smallest rational algebraic group containing its Hodge circle is a torus, with the trivial group allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\neq0\\) be a simple polarizable rational CM Hodge structure of weight \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nr_0=\\min\\{p:W^{p,k-p}\\neq0\\},\n\\qquad n=k-2r_0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has \\(n\\geq0\\), and there is a CM abelian variety \\(B\\), possibly a point, and a surjective rational Hodge map\n\\[\nH^n(B,\\mathbb Q)(-r_0)\\longrightarrow W.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(U=W(r_0)\\). Hodge symmetry makes it effective of weight \\(n\\geq0\\), with smallest index zero. If \\(n=0\\), all its types are \\((0,0)\\), so simplicity gives \\(U\\simeq\\mathbb Q\\); take \\(B\\) to be a point.</p><p>Suppose \\(n&gt;0\\), and let \\(T=J(U)\\). Its associative envelope \\(E_0\\subset\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb Q}(U)\\) is finite étale and commutative because the torus acts diagonally after splitting. Its field idempotents and all field-linear subspaces are \\(T\\)-stable. Simplicity forces \\(E_0\\) to be a field \\(E\\) and \\(\\dim_EU=1\\).</p><p>Let the Weil operator \\(C_U\\) act by \\(i^{p-q}\\). Choose the rational polarization pairing \\(\\psi\\), with the conventional weight-dependent sign absorbed, so that\n\\[\nq(x,y)=\\psi(C_Ux,y)\n\\]\nis positive definite on \\(U_{\\mathbb R}\\). The adjoint \\(\\dagger\\) sends \\(t\\in T\\) to \\(t^{-1}\\), preserves \\(E\\), and, since \\(E\\) commutes with \\(C_U\\), is also the adjoint for \\(q\\).</p><p>It fixes each primitive idempotent of \\(E_{\\mathbb R}\\): exchanging \\(e\\) would give \\(e^\\dagger e=0\\), contrary to positivity. On complex factors it acts by conjugation, since identity would give \\(i^\\dagger i=-1\\). If \\(\\dagger\\) is trivial on \\(E\\), the field is totally real. If nontrivial, a real embedding would force it to be trivial; hence there are no real embeddings and its fixed field is totally real, so \\(E\\) is CM.</p><p>The totally real case is impossible: the Hodge circle acts through \\(E_{\\mathbb R}^\\times\\), and each compact connected subgroup of \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) is trivial. All Hodge indices would be equal; their minimum zero would force \\(n=0\\). Thus \\(E\\) is CM and \\(\\dagger\\) is its conjugation.</p><p>For \\(\\Sigma_E=\\operatorname{Hom}(E,\\mathbb C)\\),\n\\[\nU_{\\mathbb C}=\\bigoplus_{\\sigma\\in\\Sigma_E}U_\\sigma,\n\\qquad\nU_\\sigma\\text{ has type }(a_\\sigma,n-a_\\sigma),\n\\]\nwhere \\(0\\leq a_\\sigma\\leq n\\) and \\(a_\\sigma+a_{\\overline\\sigma}=n\\). Choose CM types \\(\\Phi_1,\\ldots,\\Phi_n\\) with\n\\[\n\\#\\{j:\\sigma\\in\\Phi_j\\}=a_\\sigma.\n\\]\nFor each conjugate pair, place \\(\\sigma\\) in any \\(a_\\sigma\\) lists and \\(\\overline\\sigma\\) in the others.</p><p>Give \\(H_j=E\\) the weight-one type \\(\\Phi_j\\). Proposition 1, whose realization construction applies without a Galois hypothesis, gives CM \\(B_j\\) with \\(H^1(B_j,\\mathbb Q)\\simeq H_j\\).</p><p>Identify \\(U,H_j\\) with \\(E\\)-lines. Multiplication\n\\[\nE^{\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}n}\\longrightarrow E,\n\\qquad x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_n\\longmapsto x_1\\cdots x_n\n\\]\nis surjective. Over \\(\\mathbb C\\), it kills unequal embedding labels; the all-\\(\\sigma\\) tensor has type \\((a_\\sigma,n-a_\\sigma)\\) and maps to \\(U_\\sigma\\). It is therefore a Hodge map.</p><p>For \\(B=\\prod_{j=1}^nB_j\\), its source is the rational Künneth summand \\(\\bigotimes_jH^1(B_j,\\mathbb Q)\\) of \\(H^n(B,\\mathbb Q)\\). Extend by zero on the other summands and twist by \\((-r_0)\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 12, "cite"], [5, 13, "cite"], [5, 29, "cite"], [5, 32, "cite"], [5, 34, "cite"], [5, 35, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 32, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [10, 5, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [12, 34, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 34, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [15, 26, "cite"], [15, 27, "cite"], [15, 28, "cite"], [15, 32, "cite"], [15, 34, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 34, "cite"], [21, 34, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 32, "cite"], [22, 33, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [23, 32, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [24, 27, "cite"], [24, 29, "cite"], [25, 29, "cite"], [25, 30, "cite"], [26, 29, "cite"], [26, 32, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [27, 32, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [29, 32, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"], [35, 1, "cite"]]});
